1788696890 H:REGISTER-MODEL-BLUEPRINT follow-up 2 -- found and fixed the exit-89 trigger the task anticipated | DID:bisected the tsp/S overflow (exit 89, S+tsp>64) to emit_call_or_ctor's 30 pre-bound `let is_XX = if name==HASH then 1 else 0;` lines feeding a 34-way else-if dispatch (one tail expression -- the register model needs ~30 simultaneously-live join registers for that nesting depth, spilling to temp slots once the 8 window/cs registers run out; the 30 extra symbol bindings pushed real_vc from ~9 to ~39, so S alone was 31, leaving no headroom for the ~29 tsp the nesting needs); fixed per the task's own anticipated remedy by inlining `name == HASH` directly in each else-if condition instead of pre-binding a symbol per case, dropping real_vc back near the param count (S small again; the nesting/tsp cost is unchanged but now has headroom) -- this IS the exit_89_rewrite the VERDICT format asks for, recorded here since diag_exit's own position math points at the function's tail, not a single line | GOT:that fixed the specific 89 bisected at bebop.bp:~1550; chain re-run still traps 81 (frame heap exhausted) during gen2 self-compile, now bisected to complete somewhere in the small vs_mat*/vs_reg/vs_to cluster (bebop.bp ~1970-1986) -- none of those functions contain array/struct/enum literal syntax themselves, so the leak is either a carry-over from something compiled earlier that never released frame-heap pressure, or a second, not-yet-isolated instance of the same 'too many live join registers in one tail expression' class elsewhere in bebop.bp; not resolved within the session's remaining time | VERDICT:refuted -- RED continues; three real bugs found this round, two fixed and verified (fn-table overflow already covered by the prior entry; emit_call_or_ctor's S+tsp overflow), one (frame-heap trap during self-compile, cause not yet in the visible source range) still open
1788698410 H:REGISTER-MODEL-BLUEPRINT follow-up 3 -- root cause of the trap-81/exit-89 chain found | DID:implemented the coordinator's (a)/(b): hoisted vs_place_args' changed/i locals above the while bodies (L8), bounded the fixpoint loop to nargs+2 passes and the vs_break_cycle recursion to nargs+2 tries with a loud sys_exit(89) if still unplaced; verified mat_const already returns -1 on a movk-terminated constant per spec (already correct, no fix needed); then bisected 4 more genuine S+tsp>64 overflows via instrumented builds (temporary sys_write of the raw byte offset / s0,tsp0 values, reverted before the next test) and fixed each by splitting the offending fn's locals into fresh call frames: emit_body (74 locals -> keyword classification moved to emit_body_classify, 48 left), emit_call (40 -> resolution moved to emit_call_resolve), compile_fn (removed a genuinely dead first fn-table build immediately shadowed by the real one), compile_fn_at (46 -> total_saved and fact-decode moved to compile_fn_at_total_saved/compile_fn_at_facts + fw_vc/fw_alloc/fw_cshi/fw_tsp, 25 left) | GOT:each fix moved the failure to the next function rather than closing it; the last one (compile_fn_at, S=25) still hit S+tsp>64 with tsp0=90, implausibly large for that function's own complexity -- the tell: compile_fn_at calls emit_body with TEN arguments. vs_place_args occupies window indices base..base+nargs-1 but the window array is exactly 8 cells; evaluating argument 9 pushes w to 8, vs_push's cap check fires vs_park_deepest on entry 0, but entry 0 is a bare SYM tag (not REG/MULC/FLAGS) so nothing is freed and w never decreases (park is intentionally in-place); vs_push then writes PAST the array into fntab[3823..3825] (cs mask/slot cursor/cs_hi), corrupting slot bookkeeping for the rest of that compile -- that corruption is what tsp0=90 and the earlier trap 81 actually measure. This is the gap already flagged open in an earlier VERDICT (8 registers happens to equal 8 array slots, hiding it for <=8-arg calls; c55_vswindow's 8-arg construct does not reach it either) -- now CONFIRMED load-bearing: emit_body is called with 10 args from four sites (compile_fn, compile_fn_at, emit_while_stmt, emit_call), so self-compilation cannot avoid it. Correct fix is structural (args beyond the 8th cannot be pending window entries under an 8-slot array; needs a genuinely different path for nargs>8, e.g. spilling each argument past the 8th directly as it is produced, never occupying a window index) and was not designed, implemented, or verified within this session's remaining time | VERDICT:refuted -- RED continues; four more real S+tsp overflows found and fixed (safe, verified, no behaviour change beyond fn count), but the underlying window-array-capacity bug they were symptoms of is now root-caused and documented, not fixed
1788703186 H:REGISTER-MODEL-BLUEPRINT follow-up 4 -- window relocation implemented, 6 more real bugs found+fixed, gen3 still hangs | DID:implemented the coordinator's window-array-decoupling spec exactly: vs_entry_kind/p0/p1/vs_set_entry relocated fntab[3799..]->fntab[2000+3i..] (128-entry capacity, 2000..2383), vs_push's w>=8 vs_park_deepest cap replaced with a w==128 sys_exit(89) (vs_park_deepest now only reachable from vs_alloc's mask-empty fallback), check_abi.py ZONES updated (added window/window_hdr/window_cs tuples), exit-89 docs (bebop.bp comment table + docs/TRAPS.md) got the 4th reason. Rebuilt gen2, ran gen3 (self-compile of bebop.bp) -- SUCCEEDED (no trap 81/89), but gen4 (gen3 compiling bebop.bp again) crashed SIGSEGV even on a 1-line tmin.bp, proving gen3's OWN emitted code (not the compile-time logic, which gen2 runs correctly) is miscompiled. Root-caused and fixed 6 DISTINCT real bugs via a reproduce-with-gdb-then-bisect-the-.bp-file loop (never touched gen3/gen4 binaries directly, only ever fixed bebop.bp then rebuilt): (1) vs_park_to_slot's STR-to-slot encoding had `d0*32` (lands in the Rn/base field) instead of `d0` (Rt/value field) and a base constant missing Rn=15 entirely -- verified the correct encoding via `as`+objdump (`str x0,[x15]`=4177527264) and fixed all 3 occurrences (vs_park_to_slot, vs_bind's is_spill branch, vs_break_cycle) the same way; (2) vs_mat_sym and vs_bind_reg's spilled-SYM read path used `(reg-100)*1024` but the WRITE side (vs_bind) uses `(S+(reg-100))*1024` -- added the missing `+ fntab[3827]` (S) to both reads, matching vs_mat_slot's already-correct pattern; (3) the same missing-S bug in the param-prologue's spilled-parameter STORE (compile_fn/compile_fn_at's p_strw, `(j[0]-8)*1024` -> `(fntab[3827]+j[0]-8)*1024`), found via t10args regressing from 55 to 36 after fixing (2) -- write and read must agree, and (2) alone flipped which side was wrong; (4) sym_bind unconditionally called for a discard `let _ = e;`'s name ("_", hash 95) even though the caller always uses vs_pop not vs_bind for it -- this silently burned one of the 8 cs registers (or, past the boundary, forced every LATER named binding in the function to spill one slot earlier than it should) for a value nothing ever reads back; fixed by making sym_bind a no-op (no stab insert, no cnt increment, returns -1) for name==95. All 6 verified via a growing regression suite (trepro2/trepro_n4/trepro7/tdebug7/tsolo9/tfib/t10args/twhile/tarr/t2call/manyif/tloopalloc, all correct) built bottom-up from the original SIGSEGV(x19=NULL in fill_check) symptom, each one isolated to a self-contained <20-line .bp reproduction compiled with the last-known-good gen2 (never with a suspect gen3/gen4). | GOT:after all 6 fixes, gen2/gen3 build clean and pass the whole regression suite, but gen3 (self-hosted) HANGS (>2 min, 100% CPU, not a crash) on `compile`/`check` of even the most trivial 1-line program, while `size`/`version` (which skip use_expand/parsing) return correctly and gen2 (running the IDENTICAL logic, non-self-hosted) completes both `compile` and `check` on the same input instantly -- proof this is a 7th, still-unfound register-model codegen defect (not a logic bug: gen2's machine code, produced by the OLD compiler executing this exact source, is correct) rather than the 6 already fixed. gdb sampling (PC + a static x15-relative slot dump, both stable across a 1s window) traced it into use_scan (T47 `use "path"` line scanner, ~26 named locals in one fn, deeply spilled past the 8 cs registers) inside its outer `while i < strn` loop's `is_use` boolean-chain computation; two temporary instrumented iteration-count guards (on `i` up to 5*10^7 and the inner path-scan `pl` up to 5*10^6, both well above anything a real file could need) never tripped even after 2 minutes, meaning `i` is not slowly climbing toward a corrupted huge bound (which would eventually trip the guard) but is either genuinely stuck (a rebind-inside-a-while-loop write that does not stick for an 8th-registerand-later spilled loop variable, not covered by fixes 1-3 above) or the loop is re-executing a stale condition read across the bottom-tested backward branch that emit_while_stmt/vs_loop_branch compiles once and runs every iteration. Both debug guards were removed before finishing (bebop.bp is byte-identical to its state right before they were added, confirmed by diff) -- no instrumentation was left in the tree. | VERDICT:RED, fixpoint not reached. gen2 builds clean and is fully correct on every test tried (including a real 10-arg call, real self-recursion, real >8-live-cs-register functions, and all the array/struct-literal >8-window-entry scenarios the window relocation exists for); gen3 (bebop.bp self-hosted through the register model for the first time this session, now that fixes 1-3 stopped it from being corrupted before reaching this code) hangs during its own use_scan, so gen4/battery/census/word_budget/PERF/bebop.bin promotion were never attempted -- promoting bebop.bin now would be wrong even if GREEN elsewhere, since gen3 cannot even compile `fn main()->i64{42}`. window_max not measured (chain never got past gen2->gen3->hang). All 6 fixes plus the window relocation are believed correct and worth keeping (each has an isolated, still-passing regression case); the open item is squarely the 7th bug, inside use_scan's or emit_while_stmt's handling of a rebound, deeply-spilled while-loop-condition variable. No commit made; bebop.bp is the only file with real (non-reverted) changes plus tools/check_abi.py and docs/TRAPS.md; bebop.bin untouched.
