1788849826 H:the Signal 9s that end sessions on this box are Android's phantom-process killer (max_phantom_processes=32), not the OOM killer, and the repo's own defaults were the thing exceeding it | DID: main session, operator ask "зроби так, щоб не отримувати Signal 9"; no compiler change. Probed the platform from inside the proot: getprop ro.build.version.sdk=36 (Android 16), logcat says the app is untrusted_app_27 uid 10546 even though id(1) inside proot says root, /system/bin/device_config get activity_manager max_phantom_processes -> "cmd: Can't find service: device_config", settings get global -> SecurityException requires INTERACT_ACROSS_USERS, /proc/sys/vm/swappiness and overcommit_memory -> Permission denied, /proc/self/oom_score_adj writable UPWARD only (-500 refused, +500 accepted). Then hardened the two runners: tools/slot.sh SLOTS 3->1, MEM_FLOOR_MB 600->1000, in-slot PROC_CAP 70->32, a new PHANTOM_CAP=26 live ps -e ceiling a slot WAITS under (rc 95 only after PHANTOM_WAIT_S=300), and oom_score_adj=700 on the slot's own tree before exec; tools/battery.sh SERIAL defaults to 1. New docs/BOX.md carries the measurements and the two operator-only actions. | GOT: box is 7 cores (4,5,6 A78), 7.5 GB RAM / 3.0 GB MemAvailable, 5.6 GB zram swap with 1.9 GB already used, 12-14 procs idle -- so memory was never the binding constraint and the old defaults were: idle 14 + battery parallel ~40 = ~54 procs for ONE job, times SLOTS=3, against a platform cap of 32. New peak is ~27 for one serialised chain+battery. slot.sh --status reads "procs 14/26 (android phantom cap 32) memavail 2445MB"; a smoke run reports cores=4,5,6 proc_cap=32 oom=700 with affinity 4-6, rc=0. termux-wake-lock verified DELIVERED, not just rc=0: am startservice --user 0 -a com.termux.service_wake_lock com.termux/.app.TermuxService printed "Starting service: Intent { act=com.termux.service_wake_lock cmp=com.termux/.app.TermuxService }". Two levers stay with the operator and cannot be done from here: Developer options -> "Disable child process restrictions" (Android 14+, same switch as the adb device_config put, no PC needed) and Termux -> Battery -> Unrestricted. SERIAL=1 also retires the J=3 std_golden false RED recorded earlier today (12 gb_<op>_<sr>_0_1_0.* files share one BEBOP_TMP). NOT yet verified: that a full chain now survives end to end -- the A2b work in the tree is uncommitted and its chain has not been re-run under the new defaults. VERDICT:confirmed for the platform diagnosis and the script changes; the end-to-end chain under the new defaults is UNTESTED
1788854166 H:ROADMAP A2b step 1 -- lifting A2's nested-`while` hoist ban AND making vs_settle_flags honour the suspension flag it never read; the second half is the larger effect by an order of magnitude | DID: main session, no worker. Two changes in bebop.bp: (i) hoist_scan drops its is_whilekw/has_nested guard and the vals-clearing arm, and emit_while_stmt instead RELEASES the enclosing loop's 4 hoist pairs (fntab[4578..4585]) into a local osave at its own entry -- before the cs-mask-must-be-0 assertion -- and RE-TAKES them with one mat_const each AFTER its own backward branch, so the re-take runs once per OUTER iteration and never inside the inner loop; (ii) vs_settle_flags' is_flags now tests `fntab[4450] == 1` first, the suspension emit_cond_csel had been setting and clearing since A2 with NO reader, so every pure `if` settled its condition with a cset at the first arm push and emit_cond_csel then rebuilt the flags with cmp x<r>,#0 before the csel. Promoted after two GREEN batteries; census re-derived from the run, not copied. | GOT: chain gen2 52e2f158 -> gen3 == gen4 29fe5c72 (fixpoint, 110 s), promoted, post-promotion battery GREEN on ./bebop.bin 29fe5c72 (std_golden 111/0, construct parity 77/0, diag 17/0, parity 13/0 skip 1, pool_parity 5/0, ABI ok, invariants GREEN, words PASS). The compiler SHRANK 40511 -> 38800 words (-1711, -4.2 %) -- far more than the hoist could explain, and perf_fn/latest.txt shows why: the shrink is spread over ~every function that compiles a pure `if` (compile_fn_at -98, self_check -72, kw_bad_at -56, emit_body -53, emit_body_classify -52, emit_factor -48, ~20 more at -19..-34), i.e. 2 words per csel site, while hoist_scan's own -137 is the deleted guard. Kernels: k8h 40 -> 38, k5 278 -> 268, k7 317 -> 309, k7neon 320 -> 312, c46_andor 89 -> 81, c19_multi 53 -> 50, c43 131 -> 126, c70_csel 32 -> 30, c72_hoist 40 -> 38, c84_run 262 -> 261. Branch census rose exactly as the pre-written allow rows predict: bcond 1145 -> 1150, cbz 126 -> 127. New guard c73_hoistnest EXPECT=24000282 MATCHes (172 words, new construct). FALSE RED on the first battery, and it is a STRUCTURAL gate bug, not A2b: pool_parity derives PAR_COMPILE_K7_EXPECT = 4 * k7 words from bench/vs_rust/census.txt, but battery.sh runs the pool lane BEFORE the invariants lane that re-freezes that file, so on any codegen change that SHRINKS a kernel the gate compares against the dead number -- want=1268 (4*317) vs seed=1236 (4*309). Re-running the battery on the same gen4.bin after the freeze gave 5/5. rc=1 then rc=0 on a byte-identical binary; EXPECT: pool_parity is either moved after invariants or made to derive its reference by compiling k7 once serially with the candidate. NOT DONE in this line: A2b's actual ROADMAP gate is K8H <= 1.2x Rust on honest.sh and it is UNMEASURED -- word counts are not times, and the last honest row was 1.9x. VERDICT:confirmed for the fixpoint, the battery and the census deltas; A2b stays OPEN on its timing gate
1788854815 H:A2b's K8H gate could not be adjudicated because the honest K8H twin was too short to measure, not because the answer was unclear; with the instrument fixed the ratio is 1.9x -> ~1.65x and the 1.2x gate is still UNMET | DID: main session. First ran honest.sh on the promoted 29fe5c72 and got numbers ~2x faster than the 88d3f50c baseline recorded this morning ON BOTH SIDES -- Rust included, and the Rust twin's source had not changed -- so the baseline row was box state, not code. Ran an interleaved A/B instead (OLD 88d3f50c, NEW 29fe5c72, OLD, NEW, same invocation pattern, quiet box, wake lock held). Then, seeing the spread, fixed the instrument: new bench/vs_rust/kernel_reps.txt is the single source of truth for the per-kernel in-process rep count, honest.sh and tools/perf.py both read it instead of a hard-coded /100.0, and k8h went 100 -> 3000 reps in BOTH bench630/k8ht.bp and rust_once/k8h.rs. Re-ran the same interleaved A/B. | GOT: at reps=100 the K8H ratio on UNCHANGED code was 1.7x / 2.1x / 1.5x / 2.1x across four invocations and the Rust twin's own median swung 0.023 -> 0.052 ms, i.e. the instrument's spread (1.5x-2.1x) was wider than the effect a 1.2x gate has to resolve -- each run lasted only 4-9 ms, so clock_ms's 1 ms granularity plus the process's frequency ramp WERE the measurement. At reps=3000 (~120-150 ms bebop, ~70-90 ms Rust per run) the same A/B gives OLD 1.9x and 1.9x, NEW 1.7x and 1.6x -- reproducible to one decimal within each arm. So A2b's step-1 codegen DID move K8H, 1.9x -> ~1.65x, and the ROADMAP gate K8H <= 1.2x is UNMET and A2b stays OPEN on its second half (profile the remaining gap and close it or record why it cannot). Note for the next reader: absolute ms still drift ~25 % between invocations (Rust 0.022 in the OLD-first passes vs 0.027-0.029 in the NEW-second passes, identical binary), so only the RATIO within one invocation is a number; the bebop column now prints 3 decimals so a future reader can see that for themselves. k3h has the same too-short disease (100 reps = ~30 ms, ratio swung 1.2x-1.6x) and was deliberately LEFT at 100: bench_pinned.sh also reads bench630/k3ht.bp with its own older /10.0 divisor, so raising it needs that script fixed in the same commit. VERDICT:confirmed
1788855157 H:pool_parity's par_compile gates were not parity gates at all -- they compared a live run against a table another battery lane rewrites, so any codegen change that shrank a kernel went RED on a good compiler | DID: main session, bench/vs_rust/pool_parity.sh only, no compiler change. PAR_COMPILE_K1_EXPECT/PAR_COMPILE_K7_EXPECT were `4 * <words from bench/vs_rust/census.txt>`; both now come from par_expect(), which sed's the gate's OWN generated program from par_compile(4, p, N) to par_compile(1, p, N), compiles and runs it with the same $BEBOP_BIN, and returns 4x the 1-thread word count. Same binary, same embedded compiler, same code path, only the thread count differs; nothing outside the run is consulted. | GOT: RED->GREEN both ways on the promoted 29fe5c72 and on the pre-A2b 88d3f50c: 5 pass 0 fail for both, want=1236 seed=1236, and the generated par_compile_k7_serial.bp really contains par_compile(1, p, and really returns 309 (so the sed matched -- had it not, want would have been 4*1236 and the gate would have failed). Under the OLD code the same 88d3f50c run gave FAIL par_compile_k7 (want=1268 seed=1236). CORRECTION worth recording: my FIRST fix compiled the kernel with $BEBOP_BIN itself and called that the serial reference -- wrong, and the gate caught it. The pooled threads do not run $BEBOP_BIN; the test program embeds the CURRENT bebop.bp source as its compiler, so the threads run gen2 of $BEBOP_BIN. On the promoted fixpoint gen1 == gen2 and the mistake was invisible (5/5 pass); on 88d3f50c they differ (k7 317 vs 309) and it failed. Ordering was NOT a viable fix either: under SERIAL=0 the battery lanes run concurrently, so no lane order makes a census.txt-derived expectation safe. Kernel SIZE remains gated where it belongs -- census.py --check/--freeze-check in invariants.sh with census_allow.txt, and construct_parity's WORD_DELTA rows. VERDICT:confirmed
1788856906 H:A2b's remaining K8H gap is per-iteration WORDS, not the branch -- the data-dependent `if` is already a csel on both sides, and bebop's 12-word inner loop against the Rust twin's 8 is three missing peepholes (MADD, AND-immediate, UBFX), not an ISA, a branch or a register-model limit | DID: worker lane, three folds in bebop.bp, NO timing run (this lane never benchmarks). Extracted both inner loops first: bebop k8h (bench/vs_rust/kernels/k8h.bp on the promoted 29fe5c72) = 12 words mul/add/lsr/mov#1/and/cmp/add/sub/csel/sub/cmp/b.gt -- so docs/PERF.md's k8h_loopwords 14 is STALE, the re-derived baseline is 12; rustc 1.93.1 -O twin (bench/vs_rust/rust_once/k8h.rs, main+0xac..0xd4) = 8 words madd/sub/tst/csel/cmp/add/add/b.gt. (2a) vs_try_madd folds `a*b+c` BACKWARDS into the multiply's own already-emitted word by rewriting its Ra field -- MUL is MADD with Ra=xzr, exactly 31*1024 apart, as+objdump-verified -- guarded by the REG entry's `p` (producing-word index) == n[0]-1, by the word's own bits (31:21 == 1240, bit15 == 0 so never an MSUB, Ra == 31, Rd == the entry's register) and by the addend being in vs_reg's is_direct set so nothing can be emitted between. (2b) vs_try_and_imm emits `and Xd,Xn,#C` for C = 2^k-1 (N=1, immr=0, imms=k-1, base 0x92400000, as+objdump-verified) instead of mat_const + and. (2c) vs_try_ubfx collapses `(e >> lsb) & (2^w - 1)` into the shift's own UBFM word by rewriting imms alone (63 -> lsb+w-1; lsr x22,x20,#60 = 0xd37cfe96 vs ubfx x22,x20,#60,#1 = 0xd37cf296, as+objdump-verified), declining asr (SBFM 589, not UBFM 845), lsl (imms != 63) and any extract that would run off the top (lsb+w > 64). Guards c74_madd EXPECT=82837312, c75_andimm EXPECT=1477639, c76_ubfx EXPECT=2271612, all three from tools/bpref.py and all three carrying shapes that must DECLINE | GOT: chain fixpoint gen3 == gen4 7939ad7e9369c6cf683b94194d1ec90d (118 s); battery GREEN on it (std_golden 111 pass 0 fail, construct parity 80 pass 0 fail, diag 17/0, parity 13/0 skip 1, pool_parity 5/0, ABI ok, invariants GREEN, words PASS); census re-derived from that run, bebop 39340 1163 127 0 885 1738 278 (was 38800 1150 127), and both the census_allow and word_budget lines were written from a per-fn disassembly of the candidate, never copied. k8h innermost loop 12 -> 11 -> 10 -> 9, k8h total 38 -> 35 words, k7 309 -> 305, k7neon 312 -> 308; the folds also fire on the compiler itself (sha_transform -26, cli_compile -24, sha256_words -15, find_fn/collect_fns/mat_mulc -3 each, 16 more fns smaller) so the +540 net is 634 words of new folder (vs_try_madd 211 + vs_try_ubfx 201 + vs_try_and_imm 144 + vs_binop's three dispatch arms 78) against 94 folded away. Value identity re-checked at every step: k8h returns 2455774231069866078 on 29fe5c72 and on all three fixpoints, == tools/bpref.py. The loop is now madd/ubfx/cmp/add/sub/csel/sub/cmp/b.gt against Rust's madd/sub/tst/csel/cmp/add/add/b.gt -- ONE word apart, and that word is the `cmp #1` an `ands`/`tst` would absorb, which needs the COMPARISON to consume the extract (a 2-word lookback plus a condition flip in vs_cmp), not another binop-level fold. SEPARATE FINDING, pre-existing and NOT this change: std_golden's gb_pool is not idempotent across compilers in one BEBOP_TMP -- it leaves `$GBT/gb_pool_gate.gbpool` behind, and a later run with a different compiler binary makes its forked children die with trap 82 and the fold print 0. rc=0, EXPECT: -4783772994166464769 on a fresh $GBT and 0 + three `trap 82` lines on a reused one, reproduced BOTH ways on the promoted 29fe5c72 itself, so it is the harness, not the candidate. That is why chain 1 (fresh $OUT) was green on gb_pool and chains 2 and 3 in the same $OUT were red on it, and why every battery re-run in a fresh dir is green | VERDICT:confirmed for the codegen (fixpoint + GREEN battery + 9 words against the twin's 8); A2b's own gate is a TIME and is NOT adjudicated here -- the main session must take the honest.sh K8H row on 7939ad7e before the row can close
1788858343 H:A2b's gate closes -- the worker's three peepholes take K8H from 1.8x to 1.1x, reproducibly, and the K2H wobble in the same table is the instrument, not a regression | DID: main session adjudicating the worker's lane (verification by a different agent, per CLAUDE.md). Re-ran the chain MYSELF on the worker's working tree rather than trusting its numbers: gen2 da6b436a -> gen3 == gen4 7939ad7e, battery GREEN, census gen4 39340 1163 127 0 885 1738 278 -- identical to the worker's report in every field. Promoted 29fe5c72 -> 7939ad7e, then took the honest A/B (OLD, NEW, OLD, NEW) INSIDE tools/slot.sh so the two other workers' chains could not run on cores 4-6 during the measurement. | GOT: K8H OLD 1.8x and 1.8x, NEW 1.1x and 1.1x -- the ROADMAP gate is K8H <= 1.2x, so A2b is MET and CLOSES. bebop's absolute fell 0.042/0.041 -> 0.025/0.027 ms per rep (-40 %) while the Rust twin sat at 0.023-0.025 in all four runs, so the move is in the numerator where the code changed. That matches the structural prediction from 12 -> 9 loop words; the worker's own two models (recurrence 1.25x, issue 1.50x) had BOTH under-predicted the 1.65x starting point, so the agreement is a fair test they passed, not a fit. K2H moved the other way in the same table, 1.8x/1.9x -> 2.0x/2.2x, and I checked it instead of waving it off: the compiled honest twin k2ht is 75 words before AND after with an identical branch profile, and a word-level diff shows exactly TWO changed words -- `mul x1,x20,x2 ; add x0,x0,x1` became `madd x1,x20,x2,x0 ; mov x0,x1`, i.e. the word-neutral `vs_try_madd` limitation the worker reported against c32_asr, with a mov an A78 renames away. The instrument cannot resolve the 8-11 % shift: the UNCHANGED Rust k2h twin itself ranged 0.320-0.370 ms across the same four runs. So K2H is NOT called a regression and NOT called clean -- it is unmeasured, because at REPS=100 it runs 61-71 ms, under the ~100 ms floor bench/vs_rust/kernel_reps.txt now demands. rc=0. EXPECT: k2h and k3h need the same reps treatment k8h got, which is blocked on bench_pinned.sh reading bench630/k*t.bp with its own stale /10.0 divisor -- that script must read kernel_reps.txt in the same commit. | VERDICT:confirmed -- A2b MET at 1.1x on 7939ad7e; K2H/K3H rows in that table remain UNADJUDICATED until their rep counts are raised
1788860570 H:the seven architecture proposals reduce to two rows worth building, one conditional footnote and two refutations -- and the review's own most useful output is a CONFIRMED memory-safety hazard nobody was looking for | DID: main session, docs only. Turned docs/RESEARCH-BYOK-ECS-DATAFLOW-2026-09-08.md (Fable analyst, read-only, 346 lines) into ROADMAP Phase C (C1-C6) plus six blueprints, each carrying the proposal it came from, why it narrowed, a falsifiable numeric gate and a cheapest-refutation step. Verified the analyst's three self-flagged unverified claims myself before writing any of it. | GOT: net 0 ADOPT-as-stated, 3 ALREADY, 3 NARROW, 2 REFUTE -- four of the seven were already in the tree under other names (BYOK is the status quo with bebop as the bytecode: no query language, no planner, no engine process, and gb_run.bp:379-401 already compiles in-process and runs over a mapped store in a forked child; memory-as-Git is store.bp's design sentence plus B4's prev; the SoA half of ECS is gb.bp:8-10). CONFIRMED and now C1's reason to exist: gb_run.bp:246 dispatches through sys_clone(17,...) -- SIGCHLD alone, no CLONE_VM, so a real fork, and a fork keeps MAP_SHARED mappings shared -- while store.bp:146 maps prot=3 flags=1, so a kernel dispatched by a process holding a writer's mapping can WRITE the store file; the B3 gate is safe only because gb_pool.bp:229 happens to use st_map_ro. Physical isolation is conditional on the parent's mapping mode at fork, and the run-inside-fork rule was chosen for the parent's REGISTER state, never as a security boundary. CONTRADICTED: swpmu.bp:1-4's recorded perf_event_open EACCES -- /proc/sys/kernel/perf_event_paranoid reads -1 on this box today, so C5 must re-probe rather than cite it. Numbers that decided the two refutations: wasm3, the smallest serious WASM interpreter, is 64 KB = 40 % of bebop.bin and it INTERPRETS (K6's 18 ns/row would return to sqlite's class), the eBPF verifier is ~30,000 lines = 4.3x all of bebop.bp and kernel eBPF needs CAP_BPF this uid 10546 proot cannot have; racing three plans solves an estimation problem this design does not have (cardinalities exact from rp[k+1]-rp[k]) on a box where one A78 already saturates DRAM at ~12 GB/s for one core AND for three. C2's win is the one nobody had costed: a 16-byte header per object makes a 3-field record 40 % header, and the G7 size row already loses 2.5x to sqlite. C4 is bounded by two rows already measured here -- the dataflow substrate at 41x and the incremental crossover at k/N = 0.39 %. No prior art supports the headline: SingleStore and ScyllaDB both adopted Wasm as the UDF language INSIDE the query language they kept, and XRP's eBPF removes an NVMe storage-stack cost this mmap store does not pay. | VERDICT:confirmed for the two verifications and for the six rows as written; every C-row gate is a PROPOSAL pending operator decision and none has been measured
1788861228 H:A3 is refuted by its own coverage census even though it works, passes its gates and is GREEN -- the applicable set is empty in real code and widening cannot fix it | DID: main session decision on the A3 worker's finished, rebased, GREEN implementation (fixpoint 4c9e0a4e on 1ed3719, battery GREEN, 84 constructs, bpref parity over six shapes / two directions / four comparison operators / steps 1-3 / symbolic bounds / trip counts 0..19 and ~1000). Did NOT take the coverage claim on report: re-ran tools/lin_census.py myself on the promoted tree. Landed the census tool, refuted the row, preserved the implementation as docs/blueprints/attic/A3-lin-folding-on-1ed3719.patch, and corrected ROADMAP A5's dependency edge, which pointed at A3. | GOT: independently reproduced -- bebop.bp 144 while loops, 0 affine, 0 folded; selfhost/std + prelude 1476 loops, 5 affine, 1 folded, and that single fold is whileb.bp main#6, a two-iteration test loop `w < 2` doing `w <- w + 2`. The worker's wider census adds: the 12 machine-generated gb_* kernels 1093 loops -> 0 folds (the same generator B7 routes every query through), csr/sgraph/gb_run/gb_compile1/ntt/attn/snn/tq 141 -> 0, honest kernels 12 -> 6 of which 6 are the gate's own. The decisive test is that raising the accumulator cap 3 -> 64 recovers EXACTLY ZERO additional loops: of 338 non-affine RHS nodes 150 are array stores, 99 calls, 47 array reads, 42 ifs. Affine folding needs a body that is a function of the loop index alone, so any loop that reads memory is out by construction -- and every real kernel here reads memory. Cost refused: +2858 words, +7.27 % of the self-compiler, permanently (the three A2b peepholes recovered only 10 of them, because two thirds of the addition is detector and parser, i.e. branch dispatch, not arithmetic). The gates would have PASSED -- predicted k1h ~0.31x against 0.5x and k4 ~0.28-0.33x against 0.6x -- which is precisely why passing them would have asserted something false: one microbenchmark closed, 2600 real loops unmoved, and the two twins were BUILT to be unclosable (their own comments say LLVM's SCEV cannot close-form them). Corrected two of A3's own acceptance lines that did not survive contact: WORD_DELTA 0 on c07_while is impossible because c07_while IS the foldable shape (150 -> 156), and k1h/k3h/k4_loopwords cannot move because perf.py's loop_words is min(loops) and the tail loop becomes smallest. rc=0. EXPECT: if the row is ever revived, apply the accumulator-last reorder in lin_emit_fold first -- one line, 9 -> 8 words per trip, and the loop-carried chain collapses to a single MADD. | VERDICT:confirmed -- A3 REFUTED on coverage, not on correctness; the implementation is preserved and the instrument (tools/lin_census.py) is kept, because the general lesson is to ask whether a class is empty BEFORE writing the blueprint rather than after building it
1788872878 H:the 96 % of a CSR build that B2 attributed to "the store's file-backed page path" is MAP_SHARED dirty-page tracking specifically, and one flag changes it by 22x | DID: main session, R1 from docs/RESEARCH-LITERATURE-2026-09-08.md finding 1, run under tools/slot.sh on the promoted 7939ad7e. Three eliminations, one variable each, identical fold 5001253080071 throughout. (a) first-touch faulting: arithmetic only -- the ci object is 80 MB = 19,531 pages, and LANG-DB-DESIGN §0.4's own measured per-page costs give 6 ms (page-cache read), 68 ms (MAP_PRIVATE CoW) or 137 ms (first write to sparse f2fs) against an observed 18,051 ms. (b) the filesystem: same program, same scale, store file on tmpfs (/tmp is a real tmpfs here, 3.8 GB) vs on f2fs (/root/.cache). (c) the mapping mode: a scratch tree with selfhost/prelude/store.bp:146 changed from sys_mmap(...,3,1,...) to (...,3,2,...) -- MAP_SHARED to MAP_PRIVATE, nothing else -- run back to back against the unmodified binary in the same slot. | GOT: (a) REFUTES first touch by 132x against the WORST model. (b) REFUTES the filesystem, and inverts it: tmpfs fill 23,074 ms (36.5x over plain) vs f2fs fill 8,048 ms (12.8x) -- the RAM-backed store is THREE TIMES SLOWER than the flash-backed one, which also explains the 1.6x spread B2 saw (28,958/27,833/18,051), since that worker was measuring on tmpfs and its cost tracks memory pressure. (c) IDENTIFIES the mechanism: back to back, same box state, MAP_PRIVATE fill 1,239 ms / total 2,036 ms against MAP_SHARED fill 27,404 ms / total 28,285 ms = 22x from one flag, with MAP_PRIVATE landing within 2x of the plain-array arm's ~630 ms. So it is not the scatter, not the medium and not the fault path -- it is the dirty-page tracking a shared writable file mapping does on every store. rc=0. EXPECT: the store arm can reach <= 1.3x of the plain arm, per the B8a shape (build scatter-heavy structures in anonymous memory, publish in one sequential pass), and any design that keeps a MAP_SHARED RW arena under a random-write phase pays this ~22x whatever else it does. CONSEQUENCE for ROADMAP A5: 'arena image = file' must not be promoted on the strength of the read path alone -- if the arena is MAP_SHARED then every zeros() scatter in every program is on this path, not just a CSR build. | VERDICT:confirmed -- the cause is MAP_SHARED dirty tracking, isolated by single-variable elimination with an identical fold in every arm
1788874971 H:the roadmap's "one A78 saturates DRAM at ~12 GB/s, and so do three" is false -- three A78s scale linearly with zero contention, and a single-core scan uses under a quarter of the memory system | DID: main session, prompted by the operator refusing the claim as physically implausible. Wrote a four-loop probe (scratch, sequential i64 sum / sequential 32-bit-packed sum / random gather with i64 indices / random gather with packed indices, 10M elements, identical fold within each pair) and ran the sequential arm solo and then concurrently, pinned one instance per core, all inside tools/slot.sh. | GOT: solo on core 4, 28 ms for 80 MB = 2.86 GB/s. THREE instances on cores 4,5,6 started together: 28 / 28 / 28 ms -- byte-for-byte the solo time, so 8.6 GB/s aggregate and exactly 3x with NO degradation. Seven instances: 28/29/43 ms on the A78s and 82/83/84/117 ms on the four A55s, which are slow but not idle (0.7-1.0 GB/s each). So ~12 GB/s is the AGGREGATE ceiling of the whole SoC, not what one core achieves, and the roadmap row had compressed docs/LANG-DB-DESIGN.md:121 -- which says '4 cores 1.0-1.4x on streams' and 'bebop's scan is codegen-bound' -- into a claim its own source never made. The same probe also shows WHY one core is low: 33 ms for 10M elements is 3.3 ns each, about 7 cycles for a loop doing one load and one add, so the sequential scan is INSTRUCTION-bound at ~24 % of the memory system, not bandwidth-bound. The 32-bit packing arm confirms it from the other side -- 21 ms against 33 ms is 1.57x while moving FEWER bytes per second (1.90 vs 2.42 GB/s), i.e. the win came from halving the ITERATION count, not from halving the traffic. And the random-gather pair settles a separate question: 133 ms with i64 indices against 128 ms packed, 1.06x, because a gather pays for a whole 64-byte cache line however narrow the index is. rc=0. EXPECT: narrower cells (ROADMAP D4) help SCANS and do essentially nothing for traversal, so D4's justification, which cites BFS, needs rewriting; and ROADMAP B6 (multi-core) was discounted on a number that does not hold. | VERDICT:confirmed -- the bandwidth row is corrected in place, and the Phase C refutation of racing plans has its bandwidth argument WITHDRAWN while standing on the fork cost, the 32-process cap and the exact cardinalities
1788875683 H:my own R1 attribution was tighter than the evidence -- the 22x is the MAP_SHARED write path, but WHICH mechanism inside it cannot be determined on this box, because /proc/vmstat is frozen under proot | DID: main session, checking a challenge the wild-ideas analyst raised against my own commit. It argued that tmpfs measuring 3x SLOWER than f2fs is a RECLAIM smell rather than a dirty-tracking one, and proposed bracketing the run with /proc/vmstat. Ran that: sampled nr_dirty, nr_writeback, pgscan_direct, pgsteal_direct, pgscan_kswapd, pswpout, pgfault and pgmajfault every 250 ms across a 15,378 ms MAP_SHARED fill. Then CALIBRATED the instrument before believing it. | GOT: every counter flat at zero across the whole run, including pgfault, which a 10M-store scatter over 88 MB cannot leave unmoved. The calibration settles it: allocating 400 MB in python and touching one byte per page -- at least 100,000 minor faults -- moved pgfault by EXACTLY 0 (176,973 -> 176,973), and pgalloc_normal, nr_dirty, pgmajfault, pgscan_direct and pswpout likewise. /proc/vmstat under this proot is a frozen snapshot, so the discriminator does not exist here and NEITHER my 'dirty-page tracking' attribution NOR the reclaim counter-hypothesis can be confirmed. rc=0. EXPECT: the measured facts are unchanged -- MAP_PRIVATE 1,239 ms vs MAP_SHARED 27,404 ms, tmpfs 3x slower than f2fs, build-in-RAM 1,442 ms vs 16,844 ms -- and the FIX does not depend on the mechanism, which is why D1 proceeds; only the attribution is withdrawn to 'the MAP_SHARED write path'. Two further box facts from the same agent, both verified by me: security.perf_harden reads 1 while perf_event_paranoid reads -1, so ROADMAP C5 named the WRONG lever when it called swpmu.bp's recorded EACCES stale -- swpmu.bp was right and needs  from the operator; and /proc/self/pagemap is readable with clear_refs accepting 4, so soft-dirty tracking is at least addressable as a user-space alternative. | VERDICT:confirmed that the instrument is unusable here; my earlier VERDICT of 'confirmed -- the cause is MAP_SHARED dirty tracking' is NARROWED to 'the cause is the MAP_SHARED write path, mechanism undetermined'
1788877539 H:two of our own sbench rows measure the wrong thing, and the durable-commit "loss" to sqlite is a category error on BOTH sides | DID: main session verifying the beat-SQL analyst's three load-bearing claims by reading the code rather than accepting them. | GOT: all three CONFIRMED. (1) bench/tq_sqlite/sbench_sqlite.py runs the durable phase under `PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL; PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL`, which per SQLite's own documentation performs NO sync per commit in WAL mode -- syncing happens at checkpoints -- while the store arm msyncs twice per commit. So the recorded 229 us vs 62.6 us (0.3x) compares a durable commit against a non-durable one. The class-matched rows in the same table already favour the store: 1.2x against synchronous=FULL and 1.5-1.6x on group commit, and the class-matched NO-SYNC row (store st_commit = 16 cell stores plus a crc and zero syscalls, against sqlite synchronous=OFF which still issues at least one write()) has never been measured at all. (2) selfhost/std/sbench.bp:159 reallocates the WHOLE ref table T with st_alloc(base, tx, n+1, dt) and copies it on every transaction, so 8.0 of the 12.8 MB the update phase adds is harness-shaped whole-table CoW, not the store's design. (3) worse, and against us: phase_durable at sbench.bp:205-215 allocates and seals the new record p, then builds h2 from st_ref(base,h,0) and st_ref(base,h,1) -- the OLD table refs -- and commits. **The new record is never linked into T.** The row therefore times appending an UNREACHABLE object, so even the 1.2x win against synchronous=FULL is unearned until the record is linked and the block CoW is paid. rc=0. EXPECT: a class-matched no-sync row should show the store 10-100x ahead, and a corrected phase_durable that actually links will move the store's number UP by the cost of the link plus CoW; both must be measured before any OLTP claim is made in either direction. | VERDICT:confirmed -- two sbench rows are invalid as comparisons, and the fix is ~25 lines across two existing files
1788880778 H:the duplicate-scan deletion is worth -26.7 % of a cold self-compile, measured by the main session rather than inherited from the lane | DID: interleaved cold A/B inside tools/slot.sh on a quiet box (12 procs), 5 pairs, pre-dedup bebop.bin 7939ad7e against dedup 7ef614f1, each arm compiling the same bebop.bp. FIRST attempt was invalid and is recorded because the failure is instructive: without clearing the output's .becache and .use between runs, runs 2-6 were 42-67 ms cache HITS rather than compiles, and only run 1 was real -- a median over that set would have reported -10 % from five measurements of the cache. | GOT: cold, cache cleared each run: pre-dedup 2477/2494/2488/2461/2473 ms (median 2477, spread 1.3 %) against dedup 1825/1802/1830/1816/1815 (median 1816, spread 1.5 %) = **-662 ms, -26.7 %**. That confirms and slightly exceeds the lane's PROVISIONAL -633 ms / -25.4 %, and the tight spread on both arms is what makes it a number rather than an impression -- the same box drifted 17 % across an earlier worker's session on an identical command, which is why the arms are interleaved inside ONE slot acquisition. rc=0. EXPECT: the four remaining whole-source scans total ~1,213 ms of the 1,816, so scan fusion is the next lever and a per-fn memo is worth only 479 ms of it. | VERDICT:confirmed -- -26.7 % on a cold self-compile from deleting two duplicate whole-source scans, four hunks and net -8 lines, with the fixpoint md5 unchanged
1788880858 H:the MAP_SHARED write tax is the CONJUNCTION of shared and file-backed -- neither costs anything alone -- and that unblocks A5 by deleting one sentence rather than re-scoping the row | DID: A5 lane, a 2x2 factorial plus the reserve, six arms over one 512 MiB mapping with a 128 MiB working region and the SAME LCG random-store stream in every arm, medians of 3 interleaved replications with the arm order reversed in rep 2, under tools/slot.sh with taskset -c 4. Instrument calibrated before use: in-program clock_ms bracketed by shell date +%s%N (mode 1: 116 ms in-program vs 182 ms wall; mode 4: 23,305 vs 23,418), the gap being process start and the untimed fold, identical in both arms. Nothing rests on /proc/vmstat, which is frozen here. | GOT: fold 29203473000 identical in all six arms. Warm-scatter ms: MAP_PRIVATE|ANON 79 (today's arena, seed.S:55); MAP_SHARED|ANON 82 = 1.04x; MAP_PRIVATE file-backed 81 = 1.03x; MAP_SHARED file-backed 41,431; PROT_NONE reserve + MAP_FIXED PRIVATE|ANON 81 = 1.03x; reserve + MAP_FIXED SHARED file 29,068. So SHARED alone is free and FILE-BACKED alone is free, and only their conjunction is taxed -- the variable R1 could not separate, because R1 compared shared-file against private-file and never tested shared-anon. First touch is refuted by direct measurement now, not arithmetic: the penalty is LARGER warm (41,431 ms = 10.4 us/store) than cold (23,584), against a 19.8 ns/store control, i.e. 524x. It is superlinear -- 1.4x at 16 MiB, 8-11x at 32-64 MiB, 315x at 128 MiB, with the mode-4 total itself swinging 30,310 / 80,317 / 79,004 across reps -- which argues against a per-store constant such as a dirty-bit set and leans toward the reclaim hypothesis, though the fix is identical either way. The 4 GiB PROT_NONE reserve SUCCEEDS on this box (/proc/self/maps shows one ---p row of exactly 4294967296 bytes and a MAP_FIXED RW window at its base returns that base), so the blueprint's 1 GiB fallback risk row closes GREEN. Also answers D1's open question 1 from the other side: the sequential publish DOES pay the tax, 173 ms against 9 ms anonymous for 128 MiB = 19x, but at 740 MB/s absolute, so D1's verdict is unchanged. rc=0. EXPECT: new gate K9 -- warm-scatter <= 1.5x an IN-RUN MAP_PRIVATE|ANON control at lg=24 (a ratio because the failing arm swings 30-108 s, and lg=24 because below it the effect is intermittent); today 79 -> ceiling 119, the recommendation passes at 81, A5-as-written fails at 41,431. | VERDICT:confirmed -- A5 keeps the reserve, x17-relative addressing, zeros-returns-an-index and file maps inside the reserve, and loses only ; the arena window stays MAP_PRIVATE|ANON, which is what seed.S:55 already does, so the change is a preservation
1788881329 H:the compiler's four whole-source scans were not paying for traversal at all -- they were paying for str_len, and hoisting it is 2.9x on top of the day's other wins | DID: compiler lane, ten anchored replacements in bebop.bp, each asserting count==1. skip_line_comment and skip_string each called str_len(s) -- a byte-at-a-time NUL scan of the WHOLE source -- ONCE PER CALL, about 1,643 calls per pass, i.e. **555 MB of NUL-scanning to skip 337 KB of text, a 1,600x amplification**. They now take strn from the caller, with the bare names kept as one-line wrappers so ~24 cold call sites are untouched; collect_fns stops passing tpos_c = [0,0], which had been killing read_ident's slen cache. Main session applied the patch, verified the fixpoint itself and took its own three-arm cold A/B. | GOT: fixpoint gen2 == gen3 == gen4 0ae01bd3 -- reached at gen2, so the change is codegen-neutral -- battery GREEN (std_golden 111/0, constructs 83/0, diag 17/0, parity 13/0, pool_parity 5/0, invariants GREEN), census 39784 1164 123 0 890 1755 284 with bcond UNCHANGED at 1164 and cbz 124 -> 123, so no census_allow line. Cold interleaved self-compile, cache cleared per run, 5 reps: pre-dedup 7939ad7e median 2,506 ms (2486-2510), post-dedup 7649a141 1,840 ms (1834-1844), hoisted 0ae01bd3 **623 ms** (617-626) = **4.02x from the day's start**, all three arms inside 1.5 % spread. The proof was already in the A10 phase table and nobody had read it that way: 347 (collect_fns) - 299 (collect_ctors) = 48 ms is exactly the 282 extra str_len calls at 0.17 ms. Two side findings: bebop.bp has NO top-level enum, so collect_ctors' entire 299 ms was find_enum and its ctor loop never ran; and the scan_literals change repairs a real out-of-bounds read, since scan_one_lit calls slen(s,pos) and pos[1] did not exist on the old 1-cell [0]. The assigned task -- fusing the four scans -- was REFUTED by its own measurement afterwards: a probe compiler running one extra full pass measured 703 ms against a 706 ms base, a -3 ms delta against 75-85 ms spreads, while still emitting 0ae01bd3 to prove the extra passes really ran. Fusing 4 into 1 would now save ~0.6 ms of 623, i.e. 0.09 %. rc=0. EXPECT: ROADMAP A10 was refuted on a fixed floor that no longer exists and must be re-measured before it is revived or left closed. | VERDICT:confirmed -- 4.02x on self-compile in one day (2,506 -> 623 ms), fixpoint verified independently by the main session, and the scan-fusion row refuted by the same worker who was asked to build it
1788885563 H:D2's premise that the join probe needs a prfm hint to reach <=1.2x the Rust HashMap probe | DID:one binary, four probe arms interleaved, R=15 medians, n=1M, both dists, one A78 core under slot.sh -- plain / group-prefetch G=8 / same template no hint / surgical control differing from the hint arm by exactly three prfm+mov pairs, folds equal to join_twin.bp throughout | GOT:u 149/122/106/108 ns/row, z 476/376/360/364, rust probe 287/558; D2 as specified drops the probe 18.1%/21.0% (kill line >=25%), the hint itself COSTS 13.0%/3.3%, and the restructuring alone gives 28.9%/24.4% -- rebuilt on the committed bebop.bin 0ae01bd3 it is 28.4%/25.2% and 0.38x/0.65x of rust; the plain probe was already 0.52x/0.85x so the <=1.2x gate never needed D2 | VERDICT:killed -- prefetch builtin dropped (net cost, refuted by the surgical arm), group pipelining kept as bench/vs_rust/std_tests/join_twin_grouped.bp with zero compiler change, denominator reproducible via rust_once/join_hash_split.rs
