1788889084 H:the chain's perf phase died with `ValueError: invalid literal for int()` and the obvious reading was a kernel that stopped printing | DID: ran the perf kernels standalone (400 runs, 5 kernels x 2 binaries, zero empties), then perf.py's exact loop shape with the same run1 (110 runs, zero empties), then instrumented perf.py itself to print the failing command's stderr | GOT: `taskset: failed to parse CPU list: taskset -c 4,5,6` -- tools/slot.sh:101 exports PIN as a COMMAND PREFIX for the shell benches (`$PIN ./cmd`) while tools/perf.py reads the SAME name as a bare cpu number for `taskset -c $PIN` and for /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu$PIN/cpufreq/*, so every run under a slot returned empty stdout and every cpufreq read went to a path that does not exist; the empty run was on the COMMITTED 0ae01bd3, not the candidate, which is what ruled out the codegen change. perf.csv shows the silent damage: selfcompile_wall rows of 9.1/19.4/20.2/20.5 ms against a true 628.8 ms, recorded from processes that never ran (all valid=0, so no gate was fooled and no alert fired -- but docs/PERF.md is generated from these rows). Fixed in perf.py by accepting either form and keeping the first core, plus a named failure when a run prints nothing. perf then completed: 117 metrics, 12 runs. | VERDICT:confirmed -- a name collision between two tools, silent since slot.sh landed 2026-09-08 morning and invisible because the lane recipe runs PERF=0
