1788891859 H:B3's OPEN DEFECT is a missing compiler_digest check on the .gbpool read path -- or else it is A5's stub-length bug seen from another angle | DID: worker reproduced the row's own numbers on the current tree with a 4-cell table (fresh vs reused pool x same vs foreign compiler_digest, the digest varied by passing a different file as gb_pool.bp's argv[2]), fixed selfhost/std/gb_run.bp and added gate gb_pool_reuse + bench/oracles/gb_pool_reuse.py; main session then RE-VERIFIED in a clean worktree cut from committed 257e71b against the committed compiler 3a827a3f, because the lane's own before/after pair had run against another lane's in-progress binary ca69273e in the shared tree | GOT: BOTH hypotheses refuted and a third confirmed -- same compiler + reused pool traps 7x (not an identity problem) and foreign compiler + FRESH pool is golden (the check is not missing); reused+foreign gives 224 x trap 82 and fold 0, and once-used+foreign gives -367455298937407970, the exact number the C1 row recorded. The pool store was a fixed 4 MiB while a Kernel's words array holds the .bin ONE BYTE PER CELL (a 29628-byte kernel = 237048 store bytes), so the advertised cap of 48 needs 11.4 MB and one run's 12 kernels fill 2.85 MB: the second run's unconditional re-append bump-allocated past the mapping (st_alloc never checks its cursor) and killed the writer children, and the resulting lookup MISS (0) was then dispatched as a Kernel by every call site. gb_pool_size/gb_pool_room/gb_bg_have/gb_kernel_require fix all four; gate RED before (same=golden, foreign=0) and GREEN after on six cells with 0 traps. My verification on 3a827a3f: typecheck 0 findings, oracles ok=113 mismatch=0 missing=0, std_golden 113/0 with PASS gb_pool_reuse | VERDICT:confirmed -- the defect is real and independent of A5, but the ROADMAP's diagnosis was wrong and is rewritten: what was missing was a store big enough for the cap it advertises, a bounds check on the bump allocator, an idempotent rebuild ch