1788951101 H:the certificate format is the seam of Phase F and its choice decides whether the producer is replaceable | DID:the coordinator put five decisions to the operator as checkboxes -- the certificate for the decidable layer, the theory the VCs reduce to, the structural layer, the encoding, and the binding -- with a recommendation and a reason on each, and flagged that a custom format has no external producer and therefore no bootstrap; the operator chose 11.3 our own minimal format (not LRAT), 12.1 QF_ABV bit-blasted to SAT, 13.3 our own term format immediately (not Lean-exported terms, not two-layer), 14.3 both encodings with text as exchange and binary as cache, and 15.1 AND 15.3 together -- sha256 of the obligation inside each certificate plus a Merkle root over a commit's obligations -- then confirmed explicitly that Lean is a CROSS-CHECK ORACLE | GOT:the set is coherent and maximally self-sufficient, and it follows from the thesis amendment made the same day: Bebop must become what Lean is without breaking zero dependencies. The consequence, stated once and not relitigated: Lean cannot emit our format, so it is DEMOTED from scaffolding-producer to cross-check oracle -- Bebop produces the proof, Lean independently confirms the theorem, and a disagreement is a bug in one of them. No Lean-to-our-format exporter is written. The bootstrap is therefore thinner than the verification report assumed, and the elaborator that PRODUCES terms moves onto the critical path from the start rather than being deferred behind a Lean-exported-term phase. Three arguments survive the format being ours and are written into F6: hinted steps keep checking LINEAR and the checker small, which was the whole reason LRAT beat DRAT; exactly ONE encoding is normative and the other is a derived cache re-established by re-deriving rather than trusted; and the per-certificate sha256 and the per-commit Merkle root compose -- the first stops replay against a different claim, the second makes the gate one number instead of N | VERDICT:confirmed -- Phase F's heading and rows F6 and F7 are rewritten to the decided form, and what stays trusted regardless is now named in F6: seed.S, the checker's source AND binary (Thompson applies to it directly, which is what makes D5b's diverse witness load-bearing rather than decorative), the VC generator, the bit-blaster's operator definitions, and the certificate-to-obligation binding
1788951391 H:Bebop can stay first-order and monomorphic, so Phase F's kernel can be an order of magnitude smaller than a CIC kernel -- MY OWN framing, put to a Fable research agent as its central question | DID:operator ruled that closures, generics and higher-order functions are ALSO required language features, which overturns the premise mid-flight; re-aimed the research to the sharper question instead of discarding it -- can the SURFACE have all three while the VERIFIED CORE stays first-order and monomorphic, via defunctionalisation (a closure becomes a tagged variant over a finite whole-program set of shapes, application becomes a generated match plus a direct call) and monomorphisation (generics erased by specialisation before codegen); added ROADMAP row A16 recording the decision with the tensions named before the work rather than during it | GOT:docs/LANGUAGE.md:132 lists closures and generics under 'What is NOT in the language', so that line is now a defect rather than a specification; four tensions are real and named -- both transforms need a WHOLE-PROGRAM view against the one-pass thesis, whose 2026-09-09 amendment permits a second pass only 'where it buys impossibility of error' and expressiveness is not that; monomorphisation multiplies functions against the 511 cap with bebop.bp already at 291 and bin_words at 42,330; a closure needs a code reference, exactly what A5 step 1b banished, with the pointer-free candidate being ONE i64 packing a function-table index (511 fits in nine bits) with an environment cell index, the environment an ordinary arena object; and recursive or escaping closures may not defunctionalise cleanly while a HOF with a COMPUTED function argument forces a dispatch table the logic must reason about | VERDICT:open -- the decision is recorded and binding, the mechanism is not decided; if defunctionalisation holds then the logic never sees a closure and F7's kernel stays small, and if it does not then the logic must handle function values directly and the kernel grows substantially, so A16 and F7 must be decided together rather than in sequence
1788951547 H:the surface can carry closures, generics and HOFs while the verified core stays first-order and monomorphic, so Phase F's kernel can stay small -- the framing I proposed this morning | DID:operator additionally required DEPENDENT TYPES OVER AN INFINITE UNIVERSE HIERARCHY WITH HIGHER-ORDER ABSTRACTION, which is full CIC by definition; recorded it as an amendment to row A16 rather than a new row, re-scoped the defunctionalisation claim instead of discarding it, and gave the in-flight research its third and final premise with instructions to stop arguing for a weaker logic and spend the budget on making a CIC-class kernel small and un-Lean-like instead | GOT:the weakest-sufficient-logic question is CLOSED BY FIAT, not by analysis -- there is no version of 'infinite universe hierarchy with higher-order abstraction' that a first-order kernel checks. Defunctionalisation and monomorphisation survive with a narrower claim: they no longer shrink the LOGIC, because the operator wants to WRITE dependent types and the kernel must therefore carry higher-order abstraction natively, but they keep the COMPILED ARTIFACT first-order and monomorphic, which is what the translation-validation layer reasons about. The architecture this forces preserves everything already built -- Surface Bebop elaborates to CORE Bebop, which is exactly the language that exists today, plus a proof term; the existing one-pass compiler compiles Core unchanged and the kernel checks the term; Lean, Idris and Agda all do this. The property that matters: the one-pass thesis SURVIVES for Core, because the second pass lives in the elaborator, which never emits a machine word | VERDICT:open, and the scope is now the largest of the session -- this moves Bebop from a verified language to a proof assistant that is also a systems language; the honest caveat already given to the operator once and now repeated to the research from a second source is that a half-built CIC kernel CANNOT BE CHEAPLY REFUTED, which is what makes it unlike every other row in this tree, where velocity comes from killing bad directions on a number
1788951863 H:full CIC forces 'two languages, one surface' -- Surface Bebop elaborating to Core Bebop plus a proof term -- and that preserves the one-pass thesis because the second pass lives in the elaborator; MY OWN framing, and the operator rejected it | DID:accepted the rejection as correct rather than defending the wording. In Lean, Idris, Rust and Swift nobody WRITES the core, so it is a compiler phase and not a language -- but the 'core' I named is the language that ALREADY EXISTS, with its own docs/LANGUAGE.md, 86 constructs, gates and a self-hosting compiler written in it, so naming it a core would genuinely have made two languages. Rewrote row A16 to the correct form -- ONE grammar, ONE LANGUAGE.md, ONE set of constructs, with ELABORATION AS A PHASE inside the compiler producing the internal representation bebop.bp already builds plus a proof term -- and sent the correction to the in-flight research as mine to own rather than as another premise change | GOT:the substance survives and the framing does not, and the wrong framing was HIDING the binding cost: with two programs the elaborator could live in its own binary, but with one language it lives inside bebop.bp, so the 511-fn cap binds IMMEDIATELY -- 291 today plus a 200-400 fn elaborator is 491-691, over the cap in the worst case, and that is before infinite universes are handled. A1b raised the cap once already (256 -> 512 with the fntab zone relayout, verified 2026-09-07), so the precedent exists, but the choice between raising it again and restructuring elaboration within one program must be made on fntab zones and bin_words 42,330 rather than assumed. The distinction that survives and that the operator cares about: a separate PROGRAM is fine -- tcheck.bp is a program written in Bebop like gb_run.bp or the witness -- and a separate LANGUAGE is not | VERDICT:refuted as stated, confirmed in substance -- elaboration as a compiler phase is right, calling its output a language was wrong, and the correction surfaced a cap collision that the two-program framing had concealed
1788951953 H:the roadmap's thesis should state the language's purposes as they accumulate -- a store thesis, then a verification thesis appended to it | DID:operator stated the whole thing in one sentence instead -- ONE language for everything, which self-compiles, self-proves and is a database at the same time -- so the thesis is rewritten as one claim with three properties rather than two purposes stapled together, and each property is marked with its actual status rather than its ambition | GOT:the unified form is sharper AND more honest, because it forces each property to carry its evidence: self-compiling is DONE and gated (gen3 == gen4 byte-exact, an AGENTS law on every codegen commit); the database is MEASURED (G1-G8, and today C2 closed the size row to 1.14x sqlite while B4's ns/edge gate went green at 435); self-proving is Phase F and OPEN, with nothing in the tree able to produce a certificate yet. Two of three shipped, one to build. The unification also fixes a real defect in the previous wording: it said 'Everything in the plan serves those two sentences', which invited exactly the two-languages error the operator rejected an hour earlier | VERDICT:confirmed -- and worth noting for the business case as well as the plan, since 'one language that compiles itself, proves itself and is its own database' is a sharper claim than any of the three alone and is the only one of the three that no other system makes
1788952990 H:the sgraph2 twin costing four lanes a gate is expansion DRIFT, fixable by a banner or by generating the copy at gate time | DID:worker rejected both and diagnosed the ROOT CAUSE instead -- authority was decided by a FILE TEST (selfhost/std/<g>.bp wins if it exists, otherwise the std_tests copy is its own source), which is ambiguous rather than drifting; a banner targets the copy when the harmful edit is on the SOURCE, and generating at gate time makes rung (v) vacuous and turns a loud RED into a silent loss. Built a committed manifest bench/vs_rust/std_tests/GENERATED.txt making authority EXPLICIT, plus ONE implementation of the comparison shared by checker and fixer (gen_selfsrc.sh std --check/--fix/manifest) that rung (v) now calls instead of open-coding its own loop | GOT:the number that decided the mechanism -- gen_selfsrc.sh's OWN COMMENT claimed 3 local-only gate sources and there are 23, twenty undocumented with their authority resting on an absence; manifest is 101 generated + 23 local = 124, verified by the main session; the new rung catches FOUR classes where byte comparison catches one -- EXPANSION-DRIFT (and the message now names selfhost/std/<g>.bp, the file to EDIT, plus the fix command), LOST-TWIN (a deleted twin used to promote the copy to authoritative IN SILENCE), GREW-TWIN and UNREGISTERED; main session verified the exit code honestly rather than through a pipe -- rc=1 on an injected drift, rc=0 clean, and invariants.sh:59 sets fail=1 from it; typecheck 0 findings, oracles ok=114 mismatch=0 missing=0, invariants GREEN, std_golden 114/0 | VERDICT:confirmed -- and this is the third time today a COMMENT in this tree described a state that no longer exists, after L8's frame heap and gb_roundtrip's KNOWN RED
1788952991 H:the certificate format decided by the operator can be built, and cadical can be reached without adding a dependency | DID:worker reported early as asked that CaDiCaL 2.1.2 ships INSIDE the Lean toolchain at <lean>/bin/cadical and --lrat=true --binary=false gives exactly text-hinted LRAT, no workaround; built tools/certgen.py (obligation DAG -> Tseitin bit-blast -> cadical -> the format as specified, header marking it UNTRUSTED and deletable the day bebop.bp produces this format) and, unasked, tools/certcheck.py as a reference checker because a format nobody can check is not a specification; main session re-ran every case independently | GOT:shl(x,1) == x + x is 577 vars / 1794 clauses / 709 HINTED steps / 59,405 B and checks OK; (x&y)|(x^y) == x|y is 326 steps and checks OK; the INVALID shl(x,1) == x + 1 is refused rc=2 with NO FILE WRITTEN; four checker negatives all exit 2 -- corrupted hint id named by line and missing antecedent, empty clause removed, x line stripped, hints removed from one step; and the REPLAY TEST reproduced by the main session -- a certificate that checks perfectly, filed against the wrong obligation, rejected on OID MISMATCH with both hashes printed. This turns F6's central claim from an argument into executable evidence: hinted resolution is a loop with no unit propagation, no watched literals and no search, which is what lets tcheck.bp be tens of functions rather than a solver | VERDICT:confirmed -- 15.1 demonstrated rather than asserted, and the bootstrap gap lane C named earlier is now closed by an untrusted producer that is outside the trust root by construction
1788953551 H:the checker verified in its own logic is out of reach on this horizon -- multi-year on the MM0 and Milawa precedents, and nothing in this tree's velocity transfers; the 2026-09-09 design study's section 13 item 1, which I relayed to the operator as a hard limit | DID:operator rejected it as a HUMAN-VELOCITY argument and ruled self-verification a target with no specialists and no third parties; rather than defend the study's framing I looked for the structural argument FOR the ruling and the study supplies it itself -- self-APPLICATION is free, since tcheck checks certificates about its own fns because they are Bebop, so what is expensive is not the self-application but the SEMANTICS OF BEBOP IN THE LOGIC; and that semantics is already row F4, required for whole-language verification whether or not the checker is ever self-verified. Rewrote F7 to carry self-verification as a LADDER in Milawa's shape rather than a monolith, and sent the Opus verification agent the same reframing with instructions to decompose section 13 item 1 into rungs with a number each | GOT:the costly prerequisite is bought by another row, so the residual is an induction over tcheck.bp's own 137-206 fns over a semantics that has to exist anyway -- which is a different and much smaller number than the study estimated, because the study priced the prerequisite into the item that does not own it. Milawa's ladder is the mechanism that makes it incremental: not one large proof but rungs, each verified by the rung below with the whole re-checked from rung 1, which decomposes a monolith into steps that each carry a number -- and steps with numbers are this project's entire velocity engine, the thing that killed five B4 structures today for 13-26 tool calls each. Also recorded the operator's other ruling: the `ring` rule (polynomial normal form over Z/2^64, ~8-12 fns, plus two floor-division lemmas) is built even though it is risky and slow, AND the SAT route is attempted alongside rather than instead, with the measurement deciding which closes what | VERDICT:refuted as stated -- the study's 'not reachable' rested on pricing F4's cost into F7's item, and with that removed the target is a ladder whose rungs each have a number; what does NOT reduce is still to be named rather than padded into the ladder, and the Opus agent is tasked with saying which rung that is
1788953748 H:a goal can be called unreachable once one mechanism's arithmetic bounds it -- the habit this tree had acquired, and which I relayed to the operator three times today | DID:operator made it a binding law and asked for the whole roadmap swept; added AGENTS.md L23 -- nothing is unreachable, impossible or out of reach unless it has been attempted IN PRACTICE at least several times BY DIFFERENT METHODS, arithmetic that bounds one mechanism bounds THAT MECHANISM and not the goal, and a measured refutation of a specific mechanism is explicitly NOT covered and stays as it is; then swept ROADMAP.md for every instance and classified each | GOT:three claims failed the law and two of them had ALREADY BEEN FALSIFIED IN THIS TREE, which is why the law is right. D5 said two-stage DDC was 'structurally impossible, not merely slow' because the witness had no `use` handling -- and D5b then took the witness from 0 to 46 of 73 non-vacuous agreements with ONE line to emit_epilogue, and costed `use` at 60-80 lines. B4 said the ns/edge half was 'unreachable by fixing promotion alone' and candidate (b) closed it GREEN at 435 in the same row. E1's 'can never be 4x' was softened to the measurement it actually rests on, 8.6x. B4's stall half became 'BOUNDED, NOT ABANDONED', with the untried method NAMED -- incremental merge with per-run Blooms, scheduled as its own row rather than written off. Kept, because the law permits them: measured bounds on a specific MECHANISM (A3 cannot see a store-page loop, backed by a 338-node census; a forked dispatch measures 2.0 ms against a 0.1 ms gate), graph reachability in the GC sense, the cryptographic impossibility of replay under 15.1, and the quotation of the framing F7 explicitly rejects | VERDICT:confirmed -- the law caught two live falsehoods on the day it was written, and the honest forms it mandates are 'not attempted', 'attempted by <methods>, best <number>' and 'costed at <number> and not scheduled'
1788954421 H:a negative corpus plus a python twin gives the CIC kernel a number from its first commit, answering the objection that a half-built kernel cannot be cheaply refuted | DID:lane C built tools/kcheck.py (459 lines, 383 non-comment, against an estimated ~1000 -- and recorded WHY rather than banking it: it type-checks terms and declares inductives but implements NO recursors or eliminators, so any re-estimate of tcheck.bp starts from '459 lines bought everything except elimination') plus bench/kernel_neg/, 16 files; then MUTATION-TESTED the corpus with twelve mutants each removing exactly one kernel rule; main session re-ran the corpus and reproduced every number | GOT:kernel_neg 0 accepted of 13, kernel_pos 0 rejected of 3, kernel_internal 0 of 16, kernel_parity 0/16 (the honest STARTING value -- there is no tcheck.bp yet), mutation 10 of 12 caught. THE INSTRUMENT FOUND ITSELF WRONG FIRST: the initial scorer counted a CRASH as a rejection, so the mutant with the context bounds check removed raised IndexError, accepted nothing, and an UNSOUND KERNEL SCORED AS SAFE -- kernel_internal is now reported separately and fails the run. Three corpus files exist because mutation testing found the rules they pin were untested. Two survivors are requirements rather than holes: M8_app_no_pi_check is untestable in Python (indexing a tuple yields a string and conversion fails structurally) but is the DANGEROUS case in Bebop, where the kernel reads a tag from a cell and unchecked array bounds make the same removal read a neighbouring cell that may look like a pi -- so it must be pinned BY CONSTRUCTION with a tag switch whose default traps, a construction requirement discovered before the thing it constrains exists | VERDICT:confirmed -- and the cited calculus was ABSENT from the tree for the fourth time today, so it is specified in kcheck.py's docstring from the stated constraints and FLAGGED as not a transcription
1788954422 H:the CLAUDE.md toolstack table, headed 'always on -- every session, verified 2026-09-04', describes tools that are installed | DID:operator asked why graphify and mempalace are not being used; checked instead of explaining, then checked the rest of the table, then built the graphify index and tested it on a real lookup | GOT:FIVE of the table's SIX entries are not installed -- rtk, ponytail, tb and headroom are absent from PATH, mempalace's plugin is installed but its MCP server failed to connect this session and no CLI exists, and only graphify is present. graphify is worse than absent for this project: `graphify query`, the command CLAUDE.md instructs every agent to run, DOES NOT EXIST in this version (the commands are path, explain, update, cluster-only), and the index it builds contains ZERO .bp nodes -- 2615 nodes of which 172 have a recognised language and all 172 are bash, against 497 .bp files and 63,257 lines in the tree, because graphify has no extractor for the project's own language; `explain emit_prologue_sized` returns 'No node matching'. The index was built and kept anyway since 172 bash nodes cover tools/ and bench/ | VERDICT:confirmed -- the table is corrected to the measured state with absent rows struck rather than deleted, because a tool that was expected and is missing is a fact worth carrying; this is the fifth documentation-versus-reality gap today after L8's frame heap, gb_roundtrip's KNOWN RED, the 3-versus-23 local gate sources, and the toolstack itself
1788954710 H:B1's recover row is 13x sqlite because st_reopen_verify crc-scans the WHOLE object arena on every reopen, and bounding that scan makes it fast without losing the torn-write property | DID:arithmetic first, which split the scan into a load-bearing half and a PARANOIA half by the store's own ordering argument -- the arena is append-only and st_commit_sync orders msync(range) before the superblock toggle, so a crc-valid FALLBACK superblock implies every byte below its cursor was durable before the failing commit's window opened, and only [fallback_cursor, live_cursor) can be inconsistent; refuted a running superblock crc BEFORE building (checking one number still recomputes over the whole arena, and the full scan already runs at 3.90 GB/s = crc32x bandwidth) and named lazy-on-first-touch as the candidate that CANNOT keep scrash_torn at 0 because the fallback decision is made at open time; built st_verify_range/st_reopen_lo, kept st_verify as the full fail-stop check, and scanned the fallback in full ON PURPOSE | GOT:reopen + first record 18,570 -> 380 us, 48.9x, and the row FLIPS -- 7.03x slower than sqlite before, 6.95x FASTER after, against a re-derived 2,641.9 us; recover 51,334.6 -> 40,780.4 us, 1.26x only and not further optimisable without weakening the guarantee; lookup fold 23345995320 identical; scrash_torn 50 trials 0 invalid BY CONSTRUCTION since the harness tears only commit k's appended range; invariants GREEN with the new manifest rung, std_golden 114/0 with BEBOP_MEMO=0. The row's inherited numbers are BOTH wrong here -- the store's full-scan recover is 51.3 ms not 44.7, and sqlite's recover is not a stable number at all, 2,462.4 us and 5,860.8 us in two runs differing only in WAL state | VERDICT:confirmed on reopen, refuted as optimisable on recover -- what is given up is fail-stop on COLD ROT, not a recovery capability, since a crash cannot corrupt below the fallback cursor and the store has no repair path for media rot anyway
1788954711 H:a view declared over the store and maintained at COMMIT time from B4's tail delta beats recomputing it, and is expressible today without closures | DID:arithmetic first, which refuted the DENSE case before building -- at K = n the whole-view CoW is 2.70 s over the run, 6.2x the entire log row, and even segmented at the optimum S=11 it is 527 ms, 1.21x, sparing 10 pct of segments; built the small-K case instead, VW{arr i64} K=1024 bucketed by w >> 10, maintained by ONE increment per appended edge inside tl_gen's existing loop | GOT:maintained fold == recomputed == 489029643 over all 1024 buckets and STILL EQUAL after tombstones and compaction; maintenance BELOW the 3-rep noise floor with the two runs disagreeing in direction; recompute 5 ms vs view query 5.0 us = 1000x; store +8,224 B; scrash_torn 50/0. TWO TRAPS: G2's refmask must go 764 -> 1788 or st_compact never copies the view and leaves cell 10 pointing into the OLD file -- symptom a maintained fold of 3554557677048121137, the superblock magic's neighbourhood, and a TRAP on the read after compaction; and root_write was already at the hard 14-parameter limit, so a 15th argument gives exit 100 with NO MESSAGE. The lane's own arithmetic was wrong by 22x -- predicted break-even 0.052 queries, measured 1.15, because the recompute was sized against a full-graph traversal when the view's base is the delta | VERDICT:confirmed for small-K views and refuted for dense ones -- the FIFTH structure this workload has killed for assuming sparsity it does not have, and the second time in one day a prediction was right on writes and wrong on reads, which is now a line on the worker card
1788955706 H:the token-economy toolstack in CLAUDE.md is real and the reason graphify is unused is laziness | DID:operator asked why graphify and mempalace are not used; checked instead of explaining, found five of six tools absent, then FIXED the two that could be fixed rather than documenting the gap. graphify turned out to be a PYTHON package, so its extension table (extract.py:2558) and extractor dispatch (:5769) are readable -- but patching site-packages is undone by the next upgrade, so instead wrote tools/bp_graph.py emitting graphify's OWN schema and combined it with graphify's OWN documented merge-graphs. Also wrote tools/tb.py, absent from PATH despite CLAUDE.md listing it as always-on | GOT:the .bp graph is 451 files / 2,636 fns / 3,087 nodes / 22,650 links, and the merge takes the index from 2,615 nodes and 3,366 edges to 5,740 and 24,105. PROVEN by the exact query that failed before: `explain emit_prologue_sized` now returns bebop.bp L4350 with its callers and callees, verified against grep (fn at 4350, called at 5870 inside compile_fn_at). tb measured against reading bebop.bp whole (363,343 B ~= 90,836 tokens): tb s 3,028x fewer bytes, tb n 22,709x, tb h 12,977x, and tb d on the unchanged path prints NOTHING. A local free model is REFUTED on this box by measurement rather than opinion -- 1,619 MB available RAM with procs at 33/26, so a 3B-Q4 at ~2 GB does not fit and a 1.5B would compete for the same 32-process ceiling; and ZERO API keys exist in the environment, so no hosted free tier is reachable either | VERDICT:confirmed -- and OLLAMA_SETUP.md claims 'Complete, Running' while ollama is not installed, which is the SIXTH documentation-versus-reality gap today after L8's frame heap, gb_roundtrip's KNOWN RED, 3-versus-23 local gate sources, five of six absent tools, and a graphify query command that does not exist
1788955997 H:free hosted models can be routed to for mechanical worker text on this box | DID:measured first -- all six free-tier endpoints (openrouter, groq, google, cerebras, mistral, together) answer a TLS handshake from inside the proot, so the NETWORK is not the blocker; then wrote tools/freellm.py, a router with provider fallback that reads keys from the environment or a mode-600 key file and REFUSES rather than degrades; proved every branch WITHOUT sending anything externally -- --dry-run for request construction, injected responses for the parser, and a stubbed transport for fallback | GOT:--list and --probe work; --probe with no key exits 3 and names every provider and env var while reporting all six reachable; --dry-run builds groq https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions model=llama-3.3-70b-versatile bytes=144 and sends nothing; the four response branches are correct -- success returns text, finish_reason=length REFUSES as TRUNCATED rather than returning a partial answer, an empty completion refuses, an unparseable body refuses; fallback proved by stub, groq 429 -> cerebras 401 -> google success with rc=0, and every-provider-failed gives rc=4 with each error named. The ONLY missing input is an API key: the environment has zero. A local model is refuted by measurement rather than preference -- 1,619 MB available RAM against a 3B-Q4's ~2 GB, with procs at 33 against a 32-process ceiling already exceeded twice today | VERDICT:confirmed as wiring, blocked on a key -- and the harness limit is stated plainly rather than worked around: the per-worker model selector accepts four Anthropic models and nothing else, so a subagent cannot be pointed at a free provider, and ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL routes the WHOLE session including merges, which is the operator's decision and not a worker's
1788958165 H:bebop.bp's 293-fn budget is pinned by the compiler's own cap and unpinning it needs a relayout | DID:lane A found gb_pool needs FIVE fns whose transitive closure IS the compiler (emit_words_offs, collect_fns, idx_of_main, entry_stub, use_expand), so no use-level split shrinks the concatenation and the lever is the cap; then found the fn zone is FLOATING -- envelope(cnt,ecnt) = 3*cnt + ecnt + 258 with the first fixed zone at 2200 -- so the cap rises to 562 with NO relayout at all; took 560 for margin | GOT:census 42330 1194 124 0 917 1885 296, BYTE-IDENTICAL to base -- zero words, zero branches, and no CENSUS FREEZE REFUSED or WORD_BUDGET_MISSING line to quote because nothing moved; budget measured by PADDING not by reading a constant, 293 -> 341, delta exactly the cap delta since the gb side is a fixed 219; fixpoint gen3 == gen4 == 14d19805 reproduced independently, post-promotion battery GREEN. THE RAISE EXPOSED A DEFECT NO GATE COULD CATCH: fntab[2200+i] is b1_facts, the per-fn planning facts, indexed by FN INDEX and sized 512 -- raising the cap without moving it lets fn indices 512-514 write straight through b1_scratch at 2712/2713/2714, the B1 call-site counter and skip-save flag, which is a WRONG bl TARGET silently rather than a wrong value; every construct and std_test is far under 512 fns, which is exactly why the cap was never exercised. A SECOND MISS OF THE SAME CLASS, found by re-auditing after the first fix: `offs` has FIVE zeros(512) sites including cli_compile, the ordinary compile path, so widening only the first would leave every real compile of a 513+-fn program writing past the end. Guard c122_manyfns at 521 fns, plus a 546-fn program verified through cli_compile | VERDICT:confirmed -- the pin is a GATE, not the compiler, and the cheap half is free; the elaborator still does not fit (needs 431..501, i.e. cap 650..720) and the relayout to 768 is costed and lands next
1788958607 H:the fntab relayout to cap 768 needs a zeros(8192) widening and costs bin_words | DID:lane A produced the full 44-base map and verified it programmatically -- every old base in the map, every new base a mapped image or fntab[0]/[1], the map injective -- then landed it in ONE commit with the guard first; main session reproduced the fixpoint independently | GOT:fixpoint gen2 == gen3 == gen4 == 529859b9, census 42330 1194 124 0 917 1885 296 BYTE-IDENTICAL to base so no refusal line exists to quote, post-promotion battery GREEN; budget measured by PADDING at the new cap -- +256 fns compiles, +257 refuses -- so 547 against the 341 the tree was at, +206, and A16's 431..501 fits with 46 spare; the predicted 549 was 2 high because the gb side measures 221 here against 219 at cap 560, so 'a fixed 219' was 219 +/- 2. THE WIDENING IS NOT NEEDED: top index 6999 against zeros(8192) fits, so the relayout costs ~0 bin_words and the verification report's conclusion is corrected IN THE TREE, in the comment above ZONES, so the record moves with the code. Three bases are passed as ARGUMENTS rather than indices (patch_jumps(insns, fntab, 4412/4430, ...)) and a bracket-only rewrite would have missed all three. THE FIX IS A GATE, NOT A LAYOUT: check_abi.py now reads the cap out of bebop.bp and refuses any per-fn zone or array narrower than it, negative-controlled by a copy claiming cap 900 which yields 14 PERFN FAILs naming b1_facts and all five offs sites -- the exact two classes found by hand at 560. Adjacency is UNCHANGED, b1_scratch still sits immediately above b1_facts, so an overrun at fn 768 lands where it used to at 512, which is precisely why the gate rather than the layout is the fix | VERDICT:confirmed -- and one lcjit FAIL in the chain was the documented timing-flag flake under parallel batteries, PASS lcjit (11) and 114/0 on a standalone pinned re-run
1788961795 H:F3's bounds checks are the census's one row that costs emitted words | DID:lane A probed whether each WORKER-CARD hazard still reproduces BEFORE writing a diagnostic, then re-landed commit 1 on the relayout with the arrlen zone moved 4810..4937 -> 5410..5537 | GOT:the row is a HYBRID, not a words row -- where the length is fixed by the `let` AND the index is a literal, both operands are TEXT at compile time and the check discharges in the compiler for ZERO emitted words; exit 65 `index past the end`, +18 bcond which is the SAME figure measured against the pre-relayout base and is itself the evidence the rebase changed nothing but the base; fixpoint 9f098d86, constructs 93/0, battery GREEN. AND THE PERFN GATE DID NOT CATCH THE REBASE HAZARD: fntab[4810+k] under cap 768 falls INSIDE the relayout's window zone, so check_abi reported '45 constant bases, all in the map' rc 0 while the per-symbol table would have been writing through window entries silently, with exactly the b1_facts shape -- and PERFN could not see it either because arrlen is per-SYMBOL not per-fn. Membership is not enough; a base must be the base we said it was. Fixed with a REGISTERED base->zone registry, negative-controlled. Discharge is 81.7 pct IN bebop.bp but 58.4 pct deduped and 49.2 pct excluding it, so user-shaped programs pay roughly three times the rate and the bebop.bp-only figure must never be quoted alone | VERDICT:confirmed -- trap_unrep 10/29 -> 11/30, and the census row should split into unchecked-bounds-static (zero cost, closed) and unchecked-bounds-dynamic (words, open)
1788961963 H:banning cell stepping to make a `data-1` length header sound will cost A5's evidence, since c69_index_roundtrip and c92_ptrfree both prove the index model THROUGH stepping | DID:coordinator ruled the ban goes in and the two constructs be rewritten, on the reasoning that A5's claim -- every [i64] value is an x17-relative CELL INDEX and an address exists only inside the instruction using it -- does not depend on `a + k` being legal; lane A was told to stop and reverse the decision if it found a shape where the index model cannot be exercised without stepping | GOT:stepping is NOT load-bearing and A5's evidence survives BIT-FOR-BIT -- both rewrites reproduce their original EXPECT exactly, c69 3969009064380 and c92 777920, reproduced independently by the main session; the stepping was how the cells were NAMED, not the property pinned, so c69 now names the same two cells as a[3] and a[8] instead of the stepped b[0]/c[0] and c92's second name is a plain alias with deref(b, 5). The ban is ONE predicate in tools/typecheck.py, zero words, census 43010 1212 124 0 933 1894 298 byte-identical to commit 1; negative-controlled at 2 findings on the old c92 and 0 across the corpus; invariants GREEN including rung vii's own negative sample at 4; one WORD_BUDGET line owed, c69 271 -> 273, because the rewrite trades two stepping `add`s at the `let` for two index `add`s at the use where b[0]/c[0] took the index-0 fast path. The compiler CANNOT enforce this -- it is untyped at the emitter, and catching only the `let b = a + k` form (3 of 3 corpus sites) still leaves f(a + 1), and one surviving stepped value makes every header unsound, so a partial ban is worthless | VERDICT:confirmed -- and the ban closes no trap row of its own, which the lane said rather than claiming a movement it did not cause: it is the PREREQUISITE that makes the header's row closable, because a header at data-1 is unsound while arr + k exists (b = a + 1 makes b-1 a DATA cell), and with stepping gone bpref's fat Cells(off+k, n-k) becomes unnecessary rather than unimplementable
