1789177722 H:the 9 pointer-named blobs in 4286ec1 are not stray scratch files but a live defect in emit_sys_mapb | DID:decoded the 26 emitted words of emit_sys_mapb (movz x8,#56 openat / #222 mmap / #57 close, svc #0 each) and diffed the shape against emit_sys_open and emit_sys_rename, which BOTH pack a byte-per-cell path into the 8 KiB IO scratch zone below x28 and NUL-terminate it before openat; measured the 9 filenames and sizes | GOT:emit_sys_mapb has NO copy loop -- 7 words between vs_deliver and openat, too few to copy anything -- and its own comment says path is a str handle (off<<32|len), i.e. the byte-arena form, not the byte-per-cell form sys_open packs; every one of the 9 names is exactly 5 bytes with a top byte in 0x71-0x7c (a8 10 55 f8 7a, a8 40 ca 94 77, ...), i.e. a live 40-bit user-space address in little-endian whose zero upper bytes act as the NUL; sizes 64KiB/4MiB x5/8MiB/64MiB x2/96MiB match the map lengths they were created for; files created 2026-09-11 17:32, only in 4286ec1 | VERDICT:confirmed -- openat in emit_sys_mapb is handed a pointer TO a word holding an address instead of NUL-terminated path bytes, so it creates a file named by that address; fix is the sys_open scratch-pack-and-NUL path (or accept a byte-per-cell path in mapb), plus a negative construct that fails if a mapb path ever lands outside the scratch zone. Blobs removed from the index and moved out of the repo 2026-09-12; they were in no other commit
1789178253 H:F7 kernel is merely unwired, and return is usable for early exits | DID:compiled selfhost/tcheck_kernel.bp and a 5-feature language probe at 4286ec1; then pinned the cause read-only in bebop.bp | GOT:BOTH exit 101 unbound symbol -- the kernel has NEVER compiled, 0 of its 30 fns has ever executed, and kernel_parity 0/16 in tools/kcheck.py is a hardcoded string rather than a measurement; ttag/targ1/targ2 (lines 23-25) read tm_tag/tm_a/tm_b as globals while every other fn takes them as parameters; twin baseline kernel_neg 0 accepted of 13, kernel_pos 0 rejected of 3. Two language facts, both static: collect_fns is a whole-source pre-pass (defined bebop.bp:5855, called 4382/6182/6404) so FORWARD REFERENCES WORK and a fn may call one defined later; and `return` is recognised ONLY by the statement classifier (is_return 4862, is_ret 5469, cls[4] -> emit_return_stmt 5503 gated on is_item*is_ret), never in the expression path, so `(return v)` inside an if/match arm lexes as an identifier -> unbound symbol -> 101. That is why return appears in ZERO selfhost sources despite T99 shipping it: the early-exit-inside-a-branch shape everyone wants is the one shape that does not parse | VERDICT:confirmed, worse than recorded -- error propagation in the kernel must be BRANCHLESS with uid 0 as an absorbing sentinel, not early-return; the 17-return budget is far less useful than assumed. Level rule decided by the operator 2026-09-12: both rules ship, corpus runs twice
1789179076 H:st_commit_* can be called in a loop | DID:B5 step2 put 10^5 st_commit_p calls per writer inside a while body; audited every fn reachable from that body in store.bp for allocations | GOT:exit 82 SIGSEGV at N=1000 even after every visible zeros()/st_digest() was hoisted out of the loop; the audit found EXACTLY ONE allocation in the whole chain -- st_digest is `let b = zeros(256); st_str(b,s); sha256_lo64(...)` and BOTH st_commit_p and st_commit_2pc call st_digest("PartTab") internally, so every commit leaks 256 cells of frame heap; 256k cells at N=1000, 25.6M at the row gate N=100000. st_lock_p/st_unlock_p/st_lock_g/st_unlock_g/st_begin_p/st_alloc_p/st_seal/st_parttab_write_p/st_crc/st_bytes/st_pick/st_str are all allocation-free | VERDICT:refuted, and the defect is OLDER than B5 step2 -- the pre-existing st_commit_m carries the same internal st_digest("PartTab"), so NO member of the st_commit_* family is loop-safe; the existing store gate never commits in a hot loop, which is why 10^5 commits per writer is the first caller to expose it. Fix: the digest becomes a parameter computed once by the caller (st_commit_p 11->12 params, st_commit_2pc 9->10), and every per-writer buffer must be parent-allocated and sliced because sys_clone(CLONE_VM) COPIES the x27 arena cursor into each child
1789180091 H:the tree lost its fixpoint because of a codegen miscompile in the entry path | DID:diffed gen2.bin vs gen3.bin word by word, then bisected the str path with three-line programs compiled by bebop.bin vs by gen2 (whose emitter IS the current source) | GOT:gen3 is 84 words shorter and first differs at word 956, which is emit_char_fn -- gen2 has the old `mov x2,x1; mov x1,x0; ldrb w0,[x2,x1]` and gen3 the A7 handle form `lsr x4,x0,#32; add x4,x4,x1; ldrb w0,[x17,x4]`. `fn main(argc,argv){char(argv[2],0)}` prints 65 built by bebop.bin and 0 built by gen2; `fn main(){char("A",0)}` likewise 65 vs 0, so NO string is readable under the current source, not just argv. str_len("AB") gives 2 under both because it only extracts the low 32 bits and never dereferences -- the length half of the handle is right and the offset half points at nothing. emit_str mints ((128+boff)<<32)|blen where 128 is LIT0, "the byte-region start the stub copies literals into", and LIT0 occurs in exactly two places in the tree: that comment and that formula. NOTHING copies literal bytes into the arena. Two further defects in the same change: scan_one_lit writes offsets to fntab[7000+i] and lengths to fntab[7016+i], SIXTEEN slots apart, while bebop.bp holds ~100 literals, so literal 16 overwrites literal 0 length; and emit_crc32b puts the base in x1 while its loop still reads ldrb w2,[x0],#1 | VERDICT:refuted and the cause is named -- A7 step 2 (7c03111) landed the str-handle CONSUMERS and never landed the PRODUCER, so bebop.bin works only because it predates the change and every generation built from the current source is dead on arrival; the roadmap claim "A7 steps 1-3 LANDED" is false. Adding the missing copy would still not work while the 7000/7016 tables collide at 16 literals, so the invariant is restored by reverting the migration and re-landing A7 step 2 whole
1789181881 H:gb_roundtrip and gb_gen_tier0 trap 82 because of an L8 violation | DID:traced both call chains into selfhost/prelude/gb.bp and measured the loop depth of every zeros() in gb_build_pairs and gb_transpose | GOT:every one of the seven allocations sits at loop depth 0 -- gb_build_pairs lines +18..+20 zeros(cnt) x3, gb_transpose lines +3..+5 zeros(nnz) x3 and +23 zeros(n) -- so NONE is inside a while body and L8 is not the cause; these are one-shot allocations sized by nnz/cnt, and with a large graph they run off the end of the arena. Both gates crash identically under the untouched committed bebop.bin, so neither is a regression from the A7 revert | VERDICT:refuted as L8, and it exposes a worse defect than the two gates: a large one-shot zeros() leaves the arena WITHOUT hitting the named capacity trap 80 and takes SIGSEGV instead. TG-DONE row 4 requires every capacity to be a loud trap, so the arena bound check does not cover this path. The two gates stay RED until either the arena trap covers one-shot allocations or gb.bp stops buffering whole edge lists
1789183106 H:the invariants battery can be brought back to GREEN after the A7 revert | DID:promoted gen4 to bebop.bin, re-derived the branch attribution from the dispatch-arm count across 2ada1bb/7c03111/6fb9830/4286ec1, re-froze the census with --freeze, verified the 8 reverted words from MNEMONICS through as+objdump, and fixed the one real type error outside smw.bp | GOT:bebop.bin is now the 175736-byte gen4 (fixpoint fe496cda) and it compiles tcheck.bp and reads strings; words: PASS (15 literals) on an artifact assembled from mnemonics rather than .word directives, which a lane had used and which proves nothing; census.txt frozen with the +1 bcond attributed by DERIVATION -- the builtin dispatch chain in emit_call_or_ctor went 37 arms at A7 step 1 (where 1213 was frozen) to 38 in 6fb9830, the added arm being emit_sys_mapb, while the revert itself is branch-neutral (11 if arms and 2 while loops before and after); ABI ok, expansion drift=0 unregistered=0, seed .text identical, push_words 0; typecheck census 98 -> 3 | VERDICT:confirmed -- invariants is now blocked on ONE item, the 3 findings in bench/vs_rust/std_tests/smw.bp, and they are not new: the COMMITTED smw.bp calls a 9-argument st_parttab_write that exists only in the uncommitted draft inside smw.bp.use and has never existed in store.bp, so B5 step 1s committed test program has never run. It compiles anyway because an unresolved call is a RUNTIME trap 87 in this language, not a compile error -- the same reason the F7 kernel skeleton "compiled" while doing nothing
1789183798 H:F7 needs the ~170-fn kernel before anything can be checked | DID:threw away the 30-fn skeleton that had never compiled (exit 101, globals) and wrote a 14-fn kernel plus a 10-fn driver from scratch -- one flat K array, branchless error propagation because `return` is statement-only, no allocation anywhere, uid 0 as an absorbing sentinel -- then ran the whole bench/kernel_neg corpus | GOT:every one of the 16 fixtures produces a verdict, from a file that had never executed a single function. kernel_neg 0 accepted of 13 (n01 26, n02 26, n04 24, n06 20, n07 12, n08 14, n09 22, n10 26, n11 26, n12 12, n13 23; n03 and n14 report 71 = inductives unimplemented, which counts as INTERNAL not as a rejection); kernel_pos p01 0 and p02 0 accepted, p03 71; so 13 of 16 agree with the twin and all three disagreements are the same named gap. The two rules that needed no typing at all were the ones a skeleton would have missed: STRICTLY SMALLER argument ids (n12, which the undefined-node check cannot catch because ids may appear out of order) and NO SHARING (n08, a refcount per file id, unsound under de Bruijn because a node means different things at different binder depths) | VERDICT:confirmed -- ~170 functions is not the threshold for a kernel that checks something, 24 is; what the remaining set buys is inductives, eliminators and universe polymorphism, and until k_decl_inductive exists the three inductive fixtures must be reported as internals rather than passes
1789192386 H:kernel_parity in tools/kcheck.py is a measurement | DID:wired it to run the kernel binary per fixture and score agreement with the twin, then tested the metric itself three ways -- real kernel, absent binary, and a FAKE kernel that always prints 0 | GOT:real kernel 13/16 with kernel_internal 3 (the three inductive fixtures returning 71, which is scored as an internal and never as a rejection); absent binary prints "kernel_parity: 0/16 (kernel binary absent)" and exits 0 so bench/oracles/run_all.sh stays missing=0; and the FAKE always-accepting kernel drops the number to exactly 3/16 -- the three positives agree, all thirteen negatives disagree | VERDICT:confirmed, and the third arm is the one that matters: a metric that cannot fall is not a measurement. The previous value was a hardcoded 0/%d string in corpus(), which is the same class of defect as a gate whose program computes the oracle formula -- it reads green while measuring nothing
1789192648 H:measuring the arena with sys_arena_end() - sys_arena_base() gives its size | DID:ran the difference as one expression and again through two lets, then built a five-case matrix around it | GOT:one expression gives 0, two lets give 33546737 -- a MISCOMPILE in the promoted compiler. The matrix names the shape exactly: sys_arena_end() + sys_arena_base() = 16414 = base + base, so the right-hand call overwrote the left operand; str_len("ab") - sys_arena_base() = 0 instead of -8205, while sys_arena_base() - str_len("ab") = 8205 is CORRECT; str_len("abcd") - clock_ms() * 0 = 0 instead of 4 and str_len("abcd") + sys_arena_base() * 0 = 8207 instead of 4, so it is not one builtin but ALL THREE zero-argument builtins (clock_ms, sys_arena_base, sys_arena_end); 7 - sys_arena_base() * 0 = 7, so a LITERAL left operand survives and only a COMPUTED one is destroyed. char(s,i) - char(s,j) and f() - g() are both correct | VERDICT:confirmed, class DIVERGE, and the mechanism is structural rather than incidental: every builtin that takes arguments calls vs_deliver first, which makes the value stack preserve the pending operand, and a zero-argument builtin emits `sub x0,...; lsr x0,x0,#3` straight into x0 without ever delivering. So the operand already computed into the window is gone. TG-DONE row 4 says zero tolerated miscompiles; the fuzz generator never emits these three builtins, which is why 28.5k seeds on the promoted binary never produced it. Arena facts obtained the safe way: cursor starts at 8207 cells, the limit is 33554944 cells = 256 MiB, and zeros(1000) moves the cursor by exactly 1000
1789193067 H:strict positivity is the hard part of F7 | DID:wrote k_ctor_ret, k_occurs and k_arg_pos and rebuilt k_check_inductive_ctor on them, then ran all 16 fixtures and measured parity with the twin | GOT:kernel_parity 16/16, kernel_internal 0 of 16, kernel_neg 0 accepted of 13, kernel_pos 0 rejected of 3 -- n03 30, n14 31, p03 0, and the thirteen earlier verdicts unchanged (n01 26, n02 26, n04 24, n06 20, n07 12, n08 14, n09 22, n10 26, n11 26, n12 12, n13 23, p01 0, p02 0). 18 fns in the kernel plus 11 in the driver | VERDICT:confirmed, and the rule that mattered is one sentence: positivity applies to each ARGUMENTS OWN pi spine, never to the constructors top-level spine. succ : Nat -> Nat has Nat AS the argument, an ordinary recursive occurrence that must be accepted; mk : (Bad -> Bad) -> Bad has Bad in the DOMAIN of a pi inside the argument, and a domain is a negative position. A checker that walks the top-level spine instead rejects p03 with the same code it gives n03, which is exactly what the lane got before the fix. The parity number is trustworthy because the metric was falsified first: a fake kernel that always prints 0 drops it to 3/16. F7 is NOT closed -- still owed are substitution in k_infer_app (a dependent codomain is refused with 71 today), the imax arm under the operators both-rules decision, eliminators, universe polymorphism, and the LRAT certificate binding the row gate demands
1789193238 H:the invariants battery can be made GREEN in one session | DID:promoted the miscompile fix (gen4 727e47d6 -> bebop.bin, 175808 bytes), merged the B5 lane store API and its test program, and re-ran bench/vs_rust/invariants.sh --freeze | GOT:invariants GREEN. Every rung: ABI ok on all frozen bins including the new c97; census.txt frozen; typecheck census 0 findings, down from 98 at the start of this session, with the negative sample still non-zero at 4 as T48b requires; expansion 132 sources checked drift=0 unregistered=0 lost-twin=0 grew-twin=0; seed .text identical; push_words 0; words PASS. The store gate still reads 2245524994793680850 after the store.bp merge | VERDICT:confirmed. Two cautions that belong with the number: bench/vs_rust/std_tests/smw.bp type-checks and compiles but does NOT yet run -- B5 step 2 is open and mid-bisection -- so a green invariants run is not a working multi-writer; and invariants is a rung set, not the battery, whose remaining gate failures are tracked separately
1789194095 H:B5 hangs because of the spawn/join protocol, st_open, pre-spawn loops or allocation size | DID:refuted each one with a minimal probe under the NEWLY FIXED compiler -- plain spawn/join prints 7; with st_open before the clone prints 7; with a while loop before the clone prints 7; with the clone INSIDE a spawn loop prints 7; with pre-spawn padding of 1, 100, 320, 600, 1000, 4000, 16000 and 16384 cells all print 7. Then bisected smw.bp by truncation: cuts returning a marker after lock_cells, after the scratch allocations, after st_pick and AFTER THE WHOLE SPAWN LOOP all return their marker, so the parent completes; but cells[0] and cells[P] read 0 right after the loop, so the CHILD never reaches even its first sys_cond_set. acur inside smw.bp is 8210, essentially the probes 8207, so the stack address is not it, and replacing stk(acur,w) with the inline expression changes nothing | GOT:the cause is the documented live-symbol trap, reproduced in a 25-line program: adding N extra live symbols across the spawn region to a WORKING probe gives 2 -> prints 8, 4 -> 13, 6 -> 22, and 8 -> HANGS, 10 -> HANGS. smw.bp main carries about twenty live symbols across its spawn region | VERDICT:confirmed, and the B6 rows note that this is a CORRECTNESS requirement rather than a style rule is exact. Two things deserve recording beyond B5: the failure is a SILENT hang with no output and no trap code, which is the same honesty-floor hole as a large one-shot zeros() taking SIGSEGV instead of trap 80; and the lanes earlier claim that any while loop before the spawn hangs was an artifact of the pre-fix compiler -- every one of those shapes passes now
1789194935 H:k_subst works and the impredicative arm accepts the paradox | DID:checked the lane new p04 fixture by hand, rewrote it, and ran all 17 fixtures under both level rules | GOT:the lane p04 was `(lam (Sort 0) (var 0)) (Sort 0)` claimed at Sort 0 -- that is NOT a dependent application, it is Girard shape, because it needs Sort 0 : Sort 0, and the kernel REJECTED it with 24, correctly. The lane reported step 1 complete while its own table showed a positive fixture being rejected. Rewrote p04 as a real dependent application -- id : Pi (A : Sort 0). Pi (_ : A). A applied to an axiom T, checked against Pi (_ : T). T, which is reachable only by substituting T for var 0 in the codomain -- and it returns 0 under both arms, so k_subst genuinely works. Full table, 17 fixtures: under max every negative is rejected and all four positives accepted; under imax the table is IDENTICAL except n02_hurkens_shape, which flips 26 -> 0 | VERDICT:confirmed for substitution, refuted for the paradox claim. n02 is not a paradox: it is Pi (X : Sort 0). X claimed at Sort 0, which is exactly what Lean-style impredicative Prop makes valid, and Lean is consistent. The actual door to Girard is TYPE-IN-TYPE, and n01 is REJECTED BY BOTH ARMS, so the impredicative arm does not accept the paradox. It stays UNPROVEN for a different reason -- no full Hurkens encoding has been authored yet -- and a witness rejected by both arms does exist, n01 being the simplest one
1789195571 H:gb_roundtrip and gb_gen_tier0 trap 82 because one-shot zeros() buffers overflow the arena | DID:had the peak MEASURED rather than argued -- the actual cnt/nnz each gate reaches, converted to cells, against the arena | GOT:ring_chords peaks at 3 x 192 = 576 cells and random_lcg at 3 x 8000 = 24000 cells, against an arena of 33554944 cells with 33546737 free. The peak is four orders of magnitude below the limit | VERDICT:REFUTED, and the hypothesis was MINE -- I told the lane the buffers run off the end of the arena and the measurement says they do not come close. The caller-provided-buffer restructure built on that hypothesis was reverted; it did not fix the gates either, by the lane own report. Two corrections for whoever picks this up: the cause of trap 82 in these two gates is still UNKNOWN and the arena is ruled out, so look at recursion depth or a wild index instead; and these gates cannot be judged by running them bare -- std_golden.sh invokes them as `run 30 <bin> "$GBT"` with a working directory and after earlier gates have built their inputs, so a bare run traps 82 even on the untouched tree and proves nothing
1789216976 H:trap 103 is documented in TRAPS.md but missing from bebop.bp, so the compiler silently miscompiles spawns instead of refusing them | DID:grepped the source for diag_exit(...,103) and for the ASCII byte sequence of the message (diag text is stored as an i64 array, so a text grep and `strings` both miss it) -- 0 hits either way -- then compiled bebop.bp with the promoted bebop.bin and compared digests, and checked the previous committed binary | GOT:the premise was wrong and the real defect is bigger. Promoted bebop.bin = 292b8953 / 176468 B; compile(bebop.bp) at HEAD = 7c7d1f77 / 175964 B. THEY ARE DIFFERENT COMPILERS, and 292b8953 is what HEAD commits. It was last touched by e5b64f7, whose own message claims the fixpoint gen2==gen3==gen4=7c7d1f77. The previous committed binary 24b98f90 has no trap 103, so the check entered the tree inside e5b64f7's BINARY while the same commit's revert threw its source away. Every gate number in the battery was therefore measured on an artifact no source file produces | VERDICT:confirmed -- promoted 7c7d1f77 (`cp gen4.bin bebop.bin.tmp && mv`), verified it reproduces itself from source, battery 92 pass / 23 fail, byte-identical failure list to the pre-promotion baseline, so zero regression
1789216976 H:more than 8 symbols live across a sys_clone spawn corrupt the child thread (WORKER-CARD, AGENTS.md L42, TRAPS.md 103, and the 2026-09-12 journal entry that blamed B5's hang on it) | DID:built a probe family varying ONLY the number of symbols live across the spawn -- every one read after the clone, the child writing marker 42 into a shared cell, the parent futex-waiting for it -- and compiled it with the source-built 7c7d1f77, which contains no such check | GOT:13, 14, 15, 16, 18, 22, 26 and 32 live symbols ALL compile, run and return 42 rc=0. No corruption at any count. The withdrawn binary 292b8953 refuses 14 symbols in a probe with a while loop and ACCEPTS 14 in one without, so whatever it counted was not a live-symbol count at the clone site. smw.bp does still hang under 7c7d1f77 (compiles rc=0, then rc=124 at P=2 N=1000), but a probe with the clone INSIDE a while loop using smw's exact parking protocol -- sys_cond_set marker, sys_futex_wake, the `parked` inner loop on a never-woken cell, sys_exit_thread_guard -- returns 201 rc=0 | VERDICT:REFUTED. The <=8 rule is stale since A14/A14b's spill machinery, the B5 hang has a different and still-unknown cause, and the earlier journal entry blaming live symbols must not be built on
1789217115 H:the 9 store gates are RED because of a defect in store.bp's commit path | DID:bisected slayout by truncating main (stops at st_seal -> 88 rc=0; adding only st_commit -> trap 82), then split st_commit_m into its four steps by probe, then measured the object length either side of the suspect call | GOT:st_alloc lays an object out as header at off, crc cell at off+1, payload from off+2 -- the convention st_get/st_ref/st_len/st_seal all share -- but st_parttab_write wrote its 19 cells from base[pt_off+0], overwriting the header st_alloc had just written. st_len(base,pt) = 19 right after st_alloc and 1329743170 after st_parttab_write, identical to base[sb] & 0xFFFFFFFF, the superblock magic's low word; st_seal then crc'd 1329743170 cells out of a 64 KB mapping. Fixed by giving the whole PartTab family the +2 header skip (writers, the _p multi-partition writer, and the six readers) and by making st_root dereference the PartTab instead of returning superblock cell 3 raw, which had been returning the PartTab's own offset to every caller since B5 step 1 | VERDICT:confirmed -- battery 92 pass/23 fail -> 96 pass/19 fail; sround, sround_reopen, scrash and gb_pool_abi go GREEN, slayout and schain now RETURN A VALUE instead of trapping (3305316365 and 71563930700847) but differ from goldens frozen before B5 step 1 added the PartTab object to the file, so those two goldens need re-deriving from the layout rules; scompact, sevolve and sconc are still RED
1789218897 H:slayout's frozen golden 75004859 is stale because B5 step 1 added a PartTab object to every committed store, and the gate's disagreement is not a miscompile | DID:re-derived the file bytes independently in bench/oracles/slayout.py from the layout rules in store.bp -- PartTab appended after E at PT=1043 with 19 payload cells (superblock A's 16 cells verbatim, then root, used, gen), payload[15] overwritten with the crc over those 19 cells, the object then sealed with the crc over the payload as it stands after that overwrite, and superblock B's root repointed from R to PT -- with no reference to bebop's output anywhere in the oracle | GOT:the oracle prints 3305316365 and bebop prints 3305316365. PT=1043 and USED=1064 as predicted from the rules before either was run | VERDICT:confirmed -- the store writes exactly what the layout rules say, the golden was the stale side, updated to 3305316365 on that basis and slayout is GREEN. std_golden 96 pass/19 fail -> 97 pass/18 fail
1789219189 H:the "<= 8 live symbols across a sys_clone" rule is stale -- I claimed this earlier today and committed it in a18fa8b | DID:re-ran the measurement with a probe that actually SYNCHRONISES (child writes marker 42 / w+100 into a shared cell, parent spins 200M iterations before reading) instead of reading immediately after the spawn loop, and varied the KIND of live symbol as well as the count | GOT:MY EARLIER CLAIM WAS WRONG ON TWO COUNTS. First, the probe behind it had no wait -- the parent read cells[0]+cells[1] straight after the loop, so its 201 was a race, not a result. Second, the symbol KIND is what matters: with i64 CONSTANTS 32 symbols live across the spawn are fine, because the allocator rematerialises them and keeps nothing; with symbols that must be KEPT -- array handles or call results -- the boundary is exact and deterministic across repeated runs: 8 kept symbols both children correct (201), 9 ONE child silently lost (100), 11 BOTH lost (0). Same boundary for [i64] handles and for non-constant i64 (st_digest results), so it is spill pressure, not arrays. The failure is SILENT: no trap, no diagnostic, sys_clone returns valid child TIDs (measured 11370 and 11371), the children simply never write | VERDICT:REFUTED my own refutation. The documented <= 8 rule is correct, trap 103's INTENT was correct, and removing it in a18fa8b left a silent failure mode uncovered. The original journal entry blaming B5's smw.bp hang on the live-symbol trap was RIGHT -- smw carries about twenty kept symbols across its spawn. Re-landing a correct check (counting only symbols kept across the spawn, not constants) is now an open row. Docs corrected in this commit; the WORKER-CARD line "spill slots live in the shared frame, one per process, not per thread" is the mechanism and I walked past it twice
1789219593 H:B5's multi-partition _p API is implemented (steps 2-3 landed the code) and what is missing for G10 is only the threading | DID:ran the _p API SINGLE-THREADED first, two partitions, one counter object each, then read both roots back out of the PartTab and compared payloads -- no sys_clone anywhere | GOT:it was broken at the premise. st_begin_p takes partition p's cursor from st_parttab_used_p, whose no-PartTab fallback is the single-partition constant 1024, and nothing ever seeds per-partition cursors: a fresh store returned used_p(0) = 1024 AND used_p(1) = 1024, so every writer allocates from the base of partition 0's region. The probe without any initialisation returns 222222011 -- partition 0's counter reads back as 222, partition 1's value, because partition 1 wrote straight over it, and the disjointness flag is 0. Added st_init_p, which writes the initial PartTab with each partition's cursor at its own st_region_start: used_p(0) = 1048 (just past the 22-cell PartTab at 1024) and used_p(1) = 4195328 = st_region_start(1, 2, 8388608), and the same probe returns 111222111 -- both payloads intact, both roots matching what was committed, roots in disjoint regions | VERDICT:confirmed. Partition disjointness, the premise of G10, never held before this. std_golden unchanged at 97 pass / 18 fail
1789219900 H:B5 step 3's st_commit_2pc implements cross-partition atomic commit, so G10 only needs threading | DID:staged a new root in partition 0 and a new root in partition 1 single-threaded, called st_commit_2pc, then read both roots back out of the live PartTab | GOT:it committed NOTHING -- the probe returned 00, neither root moved. Reading the code confirms why: the function re-read every partition's root/used/gen out of the CURRENT PartTab, wrote those same values back, allocated a new PartTab and toggled the superblock. It had no roots argument at all and read nothing from txs except partition 0's cursor, so the transaction's new roots had nowhere to come from. A second defect underneath it: st_begin_p seeded tx[3] with the partition's current root while st_alloc_p uses tx[3] as the allocated-cells accumulator (tx[3] = tx[3] + 2 + len, same as st_alloc), so the root was overwritten on the first allocation and was never readable from a tx | VERDICT:confirmed the row was a shell. Rewrote it with a roots array -- collect the committed state, overlay each participant's root/cursor/generation, then publish ONE PartTab and ONE superblock toggle under the global lock -- and fixed tx[3] to start at 0. The same probe now returns 11. std_golden unchanged at 97 pass / 18 fail
1789220830 H:smw.bp can be made to run by getting it under the clone-spanning symbol limit, and the limit is what has blocked ROADMAP B5 all along | DID:rewrote main so that exactly FOUR symbols are kept across the spawn loop -- env, cells, acur, w -- by packing everything else into one i64 array, arrays included: [i64] values are cell indices and st_addr/st_cells are identity functions, so an array handle packs into an i64 slot exactly. The loop bound reads env[3] rather than keeping P live. writer() reconstructs base, locks, its per-writer tmp and tx out of env and runs N real transactions through st_lock_p / st_begin_p / st_alloc_p / st_commit_p / st_unlock_p | GOT:it runs. P=1 N=50 -> 50; P=2 N=50 -> 100; P=2 N=100 -> 200; P=3 N=100 -> 300; P=3 N=1000 -> 3000; P=3 N=10000 -> 30000, every one rc=0 where the old file hung with rc=124 at every shape. The fold is walked out of the arena by fold_partition and the closed form does not appear in the source (grep 0 hits); the real API is called (st_commit_p, st_begin_p, st_alloc_p, st_lock_p all present); and breaking the feature on purpose by dropping the commit takes P=2 N=100 from 200 to 2 | VERDICT:confirmed. This needed st_init_p and the st_commit_2pc rewrite underneath it -- without disjoint partition cursors the writers overwrite each other, and the cross-partition path was a no-op
1789221285 H:schain's golden is stale like slayout's, because B5 step 1 changed the store's bytes | DID:re-derived the oracle for the PartTab, then compared all three sides, then measured the store's actual pre-compaction superblock rather than deriving it on paper | GOT:the opposite of slayout. The oracle prints 71563930701023, which IS the frozen golden, and BEBOP was the odd one out at 71563930700847 -- exactly 176 = 16+32+128 low, so three of the gate's ten internal checks were failing on stale hardcoded constants. Measured the real values: used_pre 31060 (was 10060; +21*1000, one PartTab per commit), live_pre 9018 (UNCHANGED -- st_commit_m computes live BEFORE allocating the PartTab, so no PartTab is ever counted live, which is why the obvious +21 guess is wrong), post-compaction live 4049 (was 4028; the one reachable PartTab), freed 25987 (was 5008; +21*999, every superseded PartTab is freed). All four are derivable a priori from st_commit_m and the commit count, and the measurement confirmed each | VERDICT:confirmed. Gate, golden and oracle now all read 71563930701023; reverting one constant to its stale value drops the printed number by exactly that bit's weight, 16. std_golden 97 pass/18 fail -> 98 pass/17 fail
1789221693 H:smw is non-linear -- 3x10^4 finishes in a second and 3x10^5 does not finish in fifteen minutes, so something is quadratic | DID:did the arithmetic before running anything. Each transaction costs about 30 cells (a 3-cell counter plus a 2+st_parttab_cells(P) PartTab from st_commit_p), the region is arena_cells/P, and arena_cells was the hardcoded 8388608 = 64 MB / 8, so a P=3 writer should exhaust its region at 8388608/3/30 = 93206 transactions. Then tested both sides of that predicted line | GOT:N=85000 completes with fold 255000 in 7.2 s; N=96000 hangs. Exactly the predicted boundary. It is not slowness and nothing is quadratic: st_alloc_p's bound check fires sys_exit(80) when a partition runs off its region, the CHILD dies, its done flag is never set, and the parent waits for ever -- a dead thread is indistinguishable from a slow one from the outside, which is why this read as a performance problem. The root cause is that st_open maps 67108864 + 16777216 * P bytes while arena_cells described only the first 64 MB, so two thirds of the mapping did not exist as far as the region arithmetic was concerned | VERDICT:confirmed. arena_cells is now derived from the mapping; N=96000 went from hanging to 8.2 s, and G10 at its specified shape -- 3 writers x 100000 updates -- returns 300000 twice in 8.6 s
1789222794 H:G10 can be completed to its roadmap shape -- cross-partition transaction every 100th update -- now that st_commit_2pc actually commits | DID:added cross_commit to smw's writer: it takes BOTH partition locks in INDEX order rather than call order so two writers crossing in opposite directions cannot deadlock, allocates a counter on each side, and publishes both through st_commit_2pc as one superblock toggle. Found and fixed one more defect on the way -- st_commit_2pc allocated its PartTab with st_alloc_p(base, txs, 0, ...), always partition 0, so a transaction between partitions 1 and 2 read an untouched tx slot of 0 and wrote the PartTab at cell offset 0, over the superblock; a 1+2 probe returned 00 until the home partition was derived from roots | GOT:P=2 N=1000 -> 2020, P=3 N=100 -> 303, P=3 N=1000 -> 3030, each exactly P*N + P*(N/100). Full shape, 3 writers x 100000 with a cross every 100th, returns 303000 twice at about 10.5 s. Removing the cross path alone drops P=3 N=1000 from 3030 to 2970, so the cross transactions are measured and not assumed | VERDICT:confirmed. G10 is wired into std_golden with golden 303000 and passes: 98 pass/17 fail -> 99 pass/17 fail
1789223051 H:B5's last missing property, STM validation of read-sets for cross-partition transactions, can be built on what st_begin_p already records | DID:st_begin_p puts one past the OBSERVED generation of its partition into tx[5], so a cross transaction already carries its read set. Added a validation phase at the top of st_commit_2pc: for every participant (roots[q] != 0), compare st_parttab_gen_p against txs[q*6+5] - 1, and if any has moved, release the global lock and return 0 without touching the store. The publish half moved into st_commit_2pc_go | GOT:proved it FIRES rather than asserting it. A probe begins a cross transaction over partitions 0 and 1, then lets a single-partition writer commit into partition 1 underneath it, then tries to publish: the cross commit returns 0 and the interfering writer's root is still the live root of partition 1 -- both conditions, 11. The two existing 2PC probes still return 11, G10 still returns 303000 twice, battery still 99 pass/17 fail | VERDICT:confirmed, with one honest limitation: smw's cross_commit takes BOTH partition locks before it begins, so in the gate as written the validation can never fire. It is proved by the dedicated probe, not by G10. Making the cross path optimistic -- drop the second lock and rely on validation plus retry -- is the follow-up that would exercise it in anger
1789223123 H:sevolve prints 0 because of a stale constant, like schain, and its golden is correct | DID:oracle first -- HEAD's bench/oracles/sevolve.py, the frozen golden and a lane's independent rewrite ALL print -6849083777328568796 while bebop printed 0, so the golden was never the suspect. Then localised the 0: sevolve's ok is a product of five factors and a zero anywhere annihilates the fold. Factor 5 is (live == 2000 * 4 + 2003 + 5) = 10008, and st_compact now returns 10029 | GOT:the difference is exactly 21, one PartTab -- the fresh one st_compact writes at the end of compaction, which both used and live count. Constant written as 2000 * 4 + 2003 + 5 + 21 so the derivation stays visible: 8000 Q cells + a 2003-cell root + a 5-cell migration record + the 21-cell PartTab. Gate, golden and oracle now all read -6849083777328568796, and reverting the + 21 puts the fold straight back to 0 | VERDICT:confirmed. std_golden 99 pass/17 fail -> 100 pass/16 fail
1789223407 H:scompact prints 0 for the same reason sevolve did -- a guard factor comparing against a constant frozen before B5 step 1 | DID:oracle first. bench/oracles/scompact.py at HEAD does not merely print a number, it VALIDATES its model against the real store and raises: expected live 4000003, actual 4000024. Then localised the gate's guard: ok is a product of five factors and the fifth is (live == n * 3 + n + 3) with n = 10^6 | GOT:the difference is 21, one PartTab -- the fresh one st_compact writes at the end of compaction. Both the gate constant and the oracle's model needed it, and with both updated the oracle runs and prints -2246042833172211968, the frozen golden, and the gate prints the same. Reverting the +21 in the gate alone puts the fold back to 0 | VERDICT:confirmed. std_golden 100 pass/16 fail -> the last non-gb store gate is green; every remaining failure is now in the gb_* cluster plus scrash_torn
1789224211 H:the gb_* cluster is thirteen code failures with a common root cause somewhere in gb_run or the kernels | DID:a lane established gb_gen_tier0 was FIRST in run order with an empty got=, and that its store had root=0 and superblock B all zeros -- a store nothing ever committed into. Then I reproduced the harness invocation exactly, with the same working directory and argument, instead of bare | GOT:the driver WORKS. Run in /tmp/m it returns rc=0, 13 lines, last line -4783772994166464769 -- exactly its golden -- in 164 ms against a 60 s harness timeout. Run in /tmp/opencode, the harness's own directory, it traps 82 with no output. The difference is a STALE gb_gen.store left on disk from an earlier run whose size and layout no longer match what the driver opens: st_open maps a file it does not match and the trap follows. Removing it alone turned four gb gates green; removing every generated gb store and .gbpool turned eight green | VERDICT:confirmed and NOT a code defect at all. std_golden went 101 pass/15 fail -> 112 pass/4 fail, reproduced twice, and the same 112/4 comes back after deliberately planting a 64 MB stale gb_gen.store, because the battery now clears its own generated artifacts before it starts. The journal's standing warning that these gates "cannot be judged bare" was right about the mechanism and wrong about the direction: the harness directory was the poisoned one, not the bare run
1789224629 H:gb_roundtrip is the KNOWN RED its own harness comment describes -- a compiler miscompile dating to 2026-09-07 | DID:compiled it and ran it in a CLEAN directory instead of the repo root | GOT:it prints 775084997, exactly its golden, rc=0. The miscompile diagnosis is stale -- the compiler has changed a great deal since, not least today's re-promotion. The real cause is the same stale-artifact class as the rest of the gb cluster, with one twist: gb_roundtrip writes its store into the CWD, the repo root, not into BEBOP_TMP, so the blanket clean added to the battery never reached it. Its block now does its own rm -f like every store gate | VERDICT:confirmed. 112 pass/4 fail -> 113 pass/3 fail. Also recorded: bench/oracles/scrash.py now dereferences the PartTab that superblock cell 3 addresses, and it parses a healthy store correctly (10000 4231007695826602272) -- so the parser is NOT why scrash_torn reports 50 invalid reopens out of 50, and that hypothesis is refuted
1789225016 H:gb_gen_specialised is flaky because of a race or a kernel bug | DID:traced why it passed one battery run and failed the next with no code change between them | GOT:it was MY regression, introduced an hour earlier. The memo replays a gate that has already PASSed by printing its cached stdout WITHOUT executing the binary. gb_gen is a PRODUCER -- the twelve specialised kernels, gb_pool and gb_pool_reuse all read the gb_gen.store it writes -- so a replayed run leaves no store, and once I added a clean of $BEBOP_TMP/gb_*.store at battery start the file was gone for every consumer, which folded to 0 and reported MISMATCH(0). Before the clean-up the previous run's store survived and hid it | VERDICT:confirmed. The producer now runs directly rather than through `run`, so it always executes. Two consecutive battery runs both report 114 pass / 2 fail. Rule worth keeping: a gate whose SIDE EFFECT other gates consume must never be memo-replayed
1789225544 H:moving the PartTab out of the arena into the free tail of its own superblock's page fixes the scrash_torn page-tear loss and removes the 21-cell-per-commit arena growth (operator decision 2026-09-12: take the architecturally correct option) | DID:re-sited every PartTab writer -- st_commit_m, st_commit_p, st_commit_2pc_go, st_init_p and st_compact -- to (512 - sb) + 16, gave it an object header there, and re-derived slayout's oracle for the new byte layout. Then reverted every +21 constant added earlier today, since the arena no longer grows | GOT:the siting itself is RIGHT and independently confirmed: the re-derived oracle and bebop both print 749213972, and all four B5 probes still hold (two-partition disjointness 111222111, 2PC 11 for both 0+1 and 1+2, STM validation 11). But it does NOT fix scrash_torn, which is still 50 invalid reopens out of 50 -- so the "both PartTabs share one 4 KB page" explanation is REFUTED as the cause, even though it was true as a fact. And it costs two gates: gb_pool and gb_pool_reuse now disagree with gb_gen_tier0 on the same store (8492223068894816256 against -4783772994166464769), which is consistent with removing PartTabs from the arena shifting every subsequent object offset | VERDICT:partially confirmed. Battery 114 pass/2 fail -> 112 pass/4 fail. The architecture is the one the operator chose and it stays; the two consumers and scrash_torn's real cause are named follow-ups, not reasons to revert
1789226050 H:the dominant cost on this project is fixing defects | DID:went back over the whole record of the day -- the promoted binary that was not the source's compiler, the silently-lost clone children, nine gb gates that were stale files, a memo replay that skipped a side effect, two folds annihilated by a guard factor, a 2PC that committed nothing, partitions writing over each other -- and asked what they had in common rather than what each one was | GOT:they were SILENT, not subtle. In every case the fix was minutes and the localisation was hours, because the symptom did not name its origin: a dead child and a slow one are indistinguishable from outside, got= empty had three different causes that printed identically, a fold of 0 named none of its five guard factors, and a commit that wrote nothing reported success | VERDICT:confirmed, and acted on rather than written down. std_golden's gate() now turns an empty result into a named cause from the run's exit code -- sconc went from a blank got= to EMPTY(rc=80 arena exhausted: zeros crossed x28) in one line of output. tools/arch_check.py enforces six invariants inside invariants.sh: the promoted binary IS compile(bebop.bp), no nested fn, a file-size ratchet that may only go down, every gate rm -f's its store, no producer goes through the memo, and no gate or tool script discards stderr on a run
1789227104 H:the defect record of one session is representative of the whole project | DID:mined all 938 exp.journal entries by keyword class, and put a dedicated agent through HISTORY.md in consecutive 300-line chunks rather than samples | GOT:it is representative in shape and wrong in emphasis. Journal frequencies: codegen/miscompile 69, layout/off-by-N 39, concurrency 31, memory/bounds 25, silent-failure 24, stale constant or doc 24, stale artifact 21, no-op-that-reports-success 11, hang 10, unverified claim 10. The HISTORY pass found 18 defects with ZERO dropped as vague and ranked silent-failure FIRST, stale-artifact second -- so the operator's diagnosis that visibility is the dominant cause holds across the whole record, not just today. Its top recommended check was that a claim must cite something that exists, and that check immediately found 20 dangling file citations, including ROADMAP.md pointing at blueprints that were never written | VERDICT:confirmed. Nine mechanical invariants now run inside invariants.sh, and two LOUD traps went into the store itself: trap 85 when st_seal is handed a length that cannot be an object length (the 1329743170 case, caught at the line that causes it instead of dying 10.6 GB later in a different function) and trap 86 when st_open maps a store that claims more cells than were mapped -- placed BEFORE st_reopen_verify, because that walks objects and would die inside the walk. Both proved to fire: a store written at 1 MB and reopened at 64 KB now exits 86 instead of trapping 82
1789229455 H:the claim that loud failures shorten development can be measured rather than asserted | DID:added COST:<seconds from first symptom to named cause> as a required field on every journal entry from this timestamp onward, enforced by arch_check journal-cost, and recorded the two data points already in hand | GOT:sconc's empty got= cost two lanes more than 500 tool calls and several hours before the cause was named; the same state now prints EMPTY(rc=80 arena exhausted: zeros crossed x28) in one line. gb_roundtrip carried a KNOWN RED "compiler miscompile" label from 2026-09-07 that was a stale file all along -- five days of a wrong diagnosis, resolved in minutes once the artifact was suspected | VERDICT:recorded. COST measures time to CAUSE, not to fix; the repair was minutes in almost every case today and the localisation was hours, which is the whole reason this field exists COST:0
1789229819 H:scrash_torn's 50 invalid reopens out of 50 mean the store lost tear-survivability when the PartTab moved into the superblock's page, so the two-superblock alternation no longer protects a generation | DID:stopped arguing about the tear model and tested the PROPERTY directly -- wrote two generations, read back 43, then zeroed the LIVE superblock's entire 4096-byte page, PartTab and all, and read again | GOT:42. The reader falls back to the older generation and returns its value, exactly as the design intends. So the layout is sound, the fallback is sound, and the durability property the gate exists to measure actually HOLDS. Three lanes and several hours went into hypotheses about the store; one five-minute probe of the property itself settled it | VERDICT:refuted. scrash_torn is a defect in the HARNESS MODEL, not in store.bp: the model still does not produce what the real writer produces. Note the cost the relocation does carry -- a sector tear across a superblock page now damages that generation's PartTab too, where before it sat in the arena, a separate fault domain. That is survivable precisely because the OTHER page is untouched, which is what this probe proves COST:300
1789230651 H:scrash_torn's 50 invalid reopens out of 50 are a defect in the harness model, not in the store | DID:after proving the property holds directly -- zeroing the live superblock's whole page and watching the reader fall back to the older generation -- a lane diffed what the model builds against what the writer produces and found three divergences | GOT:the model still used the pre-B5 arena formula for the superblock's root cell, where B5 puts the PartTab's own offset (16 or 528); it overwrote the PartTab bytes with the OTHER generation's when applying a sector tear, a state the writer can never produce; and it set root_p to the PartTab's location rather than to the arena root the reader must walk. With all three corrected: 50 trials 0 invalid, 100 trials 0 invalid, sync-range audit 0 mismatches, and -- the line that matters -- picked generations went from "0 distinct, k-1/k pairs seen none" to "49 distinct, k-1/k pairs seen ok", so the tears are happening and the store recovers from every one | VERDICT:confirmed, and the gate is proved live rather than weakened: forcing st_sb_valid to 1, which disables recovery, drops the audit from 1000 commits logged to 2 and the harness reports the mismatch immediately COST:7200
1789232380 H:the 2-to-3 writer regression means the global superblock lock serialises commits, so B5's next step is a lock-free publish protocol | DID:a Fable research pass read store.bp and disagreed with that reading, pointing at something simpler: st_unlock_p (line 443) and st_unlock_g (line 462) each call sys_futex_wake, while st_lock_p and st_lock_g are pure sys_atomic_add spin loops and there is NO sys_futex_wait anywhere in the file. Verified by grep: exactly two futex sites, both wakes, no waits. So every commit pays two syscalls to wake a waiter that cannot exist, and under proot each is about 27 us against an 85.6 us transaction. Removed both and re-measured, best of three per point | GOT:85.6 -> 47.3 us per commit at P=1, a 1.8x improvement from deleting two syscalls that woke nobody. My serialisation reading was drawn from noise: 63 percent of each commit was ptrace-stopped syscall time. The measurement also exposed a real defect it was not looking for -- smw at P=1 went from 856 ms to a 200 s TIMEOUT, because cross_commit takes partner (p + 1) % P, which at P=1 is the partition ITSELF, and st_lock_p is a non-recursive spin lock, so every 100th transaction self-deadlocked. The battery only ever ran P=3 and never saw it | VERDICT:confirmed for the wakes, refuted for my own conclusion. After both fixes: P=1 53.3, P=2 28.6, P=3 42.6 us per commit, G10 still 303000 twice, battery unchanged at 113 pass/3 fail. P=3 is still worse than P=2, so contention is real -- but now it is measurable instead of buried under syscall noise COST:1800
1789233153 H:sys_exit emits exit_group, as its comment in bebop.bp has always said | DID:a Fable research pass read the emitted word instead of the comment. bebop.bp:1177 emitted 3531606952; assembled `movz x8,#93` and `movz x8,#94` and disassembled them per law L1: d2800ba8 is #93 = EXIT, thread-only, and d2800bc8 is #94 = exit_group. The comment above it says "raw exit_group" and has said so since it was written | GOT:refuted, and it is the costliest defect shape this project has. Inside a CLONE_THREAD child, sys_exit(80) from st_alloc_p's region bound killed only the THREAD; its done flag was never set and the parent waited for ever, so a dead thread was indistinguishable from a slow one. Proved with a forced region exhaustion, smw at a 40000-cell arena, P=3 N=5000: OLD compiler rc=124, empty, 60260 ms of timeout; FIXED compiler rc=80 in 516 ms. One word turned a sixty-second silent hang into a half-second named exit code. emit_sys_exit_thread_guard keeps #93 deliberately -- that one IS per-thread -- and gb_run's sys_exit sites are in sys_clone(17) forks where the two are equivalent | VERDICT:confirmed. Chain fixpoint gen3 == gen4 08cd9e46, promoted, self-reproducing, battery unchanged at 113 pass/3 fail, arch_check all invariants hold COST:600
1789233978 H:the store's version cell is load-bearing | DID:read it. Cell 1 is written by every writer via st_sb_write_m and read by NOTHING, so a schema divergence is silent by construction -- B5 step 1 changed the layout under nine separate readers and each found out separately over four hours, always through trap 82 several functions away from the cause. Added trap 87: st_open and st_map_ro refuse a store whose version they do not recognise | GOT:proved it fires, and the proof took two attempts, which is the lesson. Patching the version cell alone does NOT trip it: st_sb_valid then rejects that superblock and st_pick silently takes the other one, so the store reads fine and the test says nothing. Only patching the version AND recomputing the superblock crc over cells 0..14 produces a state a real divergence would produce -- and then rc=87. A negative test that does not first make itself REACHABLE proves nothing, and I made this same mistake on trap 86 an hour earlier | VERDICT:confirmed. Also removed a zeros(256) a lane had put inside st_map_ro to get scratch for st_pick: st_crc ignores its tmp argument entirely, so no scratch is needed, and an allocation in a function readers call in loops is the exact per-call arena growth that cost this project a day. All six store gates unchanged: 749213972, -8475951406600543408, 71563930701023, -6849083777328568796, -2246042833172211968, smw 3030 COST:120
1789234991 H:gb_pool's store is corrupt because the producer writes a bad PartTab -- reported by a lane from tools/stdump.py, which showed crc_ok=False and root_p=0 | DID:did not accept the tool's word. Read the PartTab payload directly with struct.unpack: payload[16] = 44895, payload[17] = 44902, payload[18] = 1 -- a plausible root offset just under the superblock's used, the used itself, and generation 1. The store is HEALTHY | GOT:refuted, and the defect is in the INSTRUMENT. storelib.parttab read its 19 cells from parttab_off instead of parttab_off + 2, so every payload index landed two cells early and root_p came back as the superblock copy's cell 14, which is always 0; and it computed the crc over the 16-cell superblock copy where st_seal computes it over all 19 payload cells. That is the SAME off-by-two the store itself suffered this morning, now in the tool built to diagnose it. Fixed both: gb_gen's store reads crc_ok=True root_p=44895, slayout reads crc_ok=True root_p=1029 and status OK | VERDICT:refuted. A diagnostic tool is code and carries the defect classes of its subject; the number it prints is a claim, not evidence, until something independent agrees with it COST:900
1789236600 H:gb_pool and gb_pool_reuse are red because the PartTab relocation shifted every object offset, and a lane reported gb_pool_test.bin exits 0 printing nothing | DID:did not accept either claim. Regenerated the pooled build by hand and ran the SAME binary against two directories: a warm $GBT holding gb_gen.store, and a cold one without it | GOT:refuted, twice over. Warm returns -4783772994166464769, the golden, exactly; cold dies rc=82 SIGSEGV. So the binary was never silent and the offsets were never wrong -- the lane had run it in a cold directory. The real defect is that a missing INPUT FILE was reported as a wild memory access, sending the reader into the allocator to look for a bug that is not there. run()'s GUARD covered only the .bin and its own L12 comment names the other half of the hazard without implementing it | VERDICT:refuted. need_file() now guards all five producer-artifact consumers and arch_check prereq-guard (law L24) makes it self-policing at ratchet 0. The new guard immediately found a REAL instance of an old class: the gb_bfs_gen.store and gb_mbfs_gen.store producers still ran through the memo, so a replay would print cached stdout and never write the store -- the same defect gb_gen was moved off `run` for. Battery 113 pass/3 fail -> 115 pass/1 fail COST:1200
1789236900 H:sconc's trap 80 is the whole of its problem, and fixing the arena exhaustion turns the gate green | DID:applied the hoisted-digest fix Opus research derived statically (st_digest opens zeros(256) per commit, a child has 8 MiB, 8 MiB/2048 B = 4096 commits, sconc does 10^4), then read the number that came back instead of assuming | GOT:partially confirmed, and two more defects underneath. The trap went away and the fold read -40000000000, which decodes as bad=40000 with gen and every counter ALREADY CORRECT -- the writers were perfect and all 40000 reads were wrong. read_sum took snap[3] as the object root, but B5 step 1 put the PartTab there and the root lives inside it; st_root does that dereference and this reader never learned to, so it handed a PartTab to st_ref as if it were the R object. That is my own layout change, unpropagated. Fixing it dropped bad to single digits -- a real ABA: the superblock alternation reuses each page every OTHER commit, so a writer overwrites the snapshot a slow reader is still walking, invisible because the overwritten PartTab passes its crc perfectly well, just for a later generation | VERDICT:confirmed after three layers. st_snap_begin/st_snap_end are the two halves of a seqlock and live in the STORE, not the benchmark -- validating what you followed is a store concern. read_sum splits into read_once plus a bounded retry so a moved snapshot is a RETRY, not an inconsistency; counting it as one blames the store for the reader being slow. Measured 40000/40000/40000, and PASS sconc (40000) in the battery. 116 pass, 1 fail -- every pre-existing gate green; the one red is b6core, added in the same commit, and it is red at ok=0 for a correctness reason rather than for its deliberately-unreachable thresholds COST:2400
1789237200 H:the ratchet file is a set of enforced bounds | DID:checked whether anything actually reads each key, after an Opus telemetry research pass noticed max_unbounded_waits looked enforced and was not | GOT:confirmed for 21 of 22 keys and refuted for that one -- it had sat there bounding nothing, which is the law_manifest failure mode one layer down: an enforcement pointing at nothing instead of prose pointing at nothing. Added arch_check ratchet-orphan (law L25) so a key no check reads is a hard failure, then ADOPTED the orphan with arch_check unbounded-wait, which flags a while-loop spinning on cells/base/locks with no trap 89, no counter bound and no futex park. The tree measures 0, so the number was true all along and only unverified | VERDICT:confirmed. Also closed the mirror of an existing gap: check 14 rejected a law with no enforcement but accepted a manifest row naming a law that does not exist, and I had just written exactly such a row. Both directions now fail COST:0
1789244400 H:c70_qdsl and c70_qdsl_neg are red because bebop miscompiles them -- a second live miscompile next to the zero-arg-builtin one | DID:did not accept the framing. Ran both sides on the SAME source: `python3 tools/bpref.py` against `seed bebop.bin compile` + run, then shrank the disagreement by hand instead of bisecting the 339-line parser | GOT:refuted, and the oracle is the guilty party. Shrunk to five lines: `fn poke(ap: [i64]) -> i64 { let _ = ap[0] = 9; 0 } fn main() -> i64 { let ap = zeros(1); let _ = poke(ap); ap[0] }` gives 0 from bpref and 9 from bebop.bin. tools/bpref.py's `set` node did `arr = list(arr); arr[i] = v; env[name] = arr` -- it rebound the name to a PRIVATE COPY, so a callee's write into an `[i64]` parameter was invisible to its caller, and two names for one array diverged after the first write. `[i64]` is a handle (a cell index), and `Cells.__setitem__` already wrote through to the shared arena, so the copy defeated the one class that had it right. Two more copies of the same line sat in the qdsl_alloc / qdsl_set shims | VERDICT:confirmed as an ORACLE defect, not a compiler defect. This oracle is the fuzz DIVERGE judge and the source of every hand-derived EXPECT, so it decides what "zero tolerated miscompiles" means. Fixed by writing through; bpref and bebop.bin now agree on c70_qdsl (41000) and c70_qdsl_neg (1), the six probes, and 300 fresh fuzz seeds (OK=300, DIVERGE=0, rate 7.3/s, bin 08cd9e46) COST:0
1789244500 H:construct parity is green at HEAD | DID:ran bench/vs_rust/construct_parity.sh, which nothing had run since c99cddb, and read every failure instead of the count | GOT:refuted. pass=95 fail=9. Three of the nine were holes, not regressions: c70_qdsl and c70_qdsl_neg (added by c99cddb) and neg/read_before_assign (added by 4286ec1) have NO EXPECT row, so the gate has been red on them for days while everything upstream reported green. Six were frozen artifacts left in the tree by a session that compiled with an unpromoted binary. read_before_assign claims exit 105 "read before assignment" via an identifier-hash collision, but `abcdefg` and `abcdefh` do not collide: bebop exits 101 and bpref raises KeyError, i.e. the file is a duplicate of c93_unbound and the exit-105 path is covered by NOTHING. c70_qdsl_neg is worse: both sides agree on 1, which means qdsl_parse ACCEPTED `invalid query that should fail` -- qdsl_query writes `let _ = if ok == 0 then 0 else 0;` wherever an early return was meant, so the ok flag is computed and never acted on | VERDICT:confirmed. Three EXPECT rows derived and written with their derivations; parity is 104/104. The B7 parser defect is real and stays open on the B7 row -- the gate measures bebop-vs-oracle parity, and parity holds at a wrong number COST:0
1789246800 H:HEAD ships the compiler its source produces | DID:ran tools/arch_check.py on an inherited working tree before touching anything, then chained bebop.bp to a fixpoint instead of trusting either artifact | GOT:refuted, with THREE live digests at once. bebop.bin was 08cd9e46, compile(bebop.bp) was 2822d7b5, and the fixpoint gen3 == gen4 was 1d1e634f. 05fcc0f had edited bebop.bp (seed stub word 7, st[7] = 3510649756, a sub x28,x28,#16,lsl#12 reserving 64 KiB of telemetry) and never re-promoted, so every number measured after it was measured against an artifact no source in the tree produces. The tell was 85 frozen construct artifacts in the working tree differing from HEAD by exactly ONE word each at byte 64 -- not corruption: they were correct for the SOURCE's compiler and wrong for the promoted one | VERDICT:confirmed and closed. Promoted gen4; arch_check artifact-identity now reads bebop.bin == compile(bebop.bp) == 1d1e634f; battery 117/117 std_golden, construct parity 104/104, invariants GREEN. Second occurrence of this exact class (a18fa8b was the first), which is why it is now tooling and not vigilance COST:520
1789246900 H:the guards that exist are the guards that run | DID:stopped believing four of them and ran each one instead | GOT:refuted four times over, and every one had been silently passing. (1) tools/hooks/pre-commit in the REPO is 3710 bytes; .git/hooks/pre-commit, the one git actually executes, was a 1367-byte older copy with Guard 2 missing -- nothing installs or compares them. (2) The new manifest guard checked that compilers/MANIFEST.tsv EXISTS, not that the staged digest has a row, and resolved the path from bebop-lang/ while the hook runs at the repo root -- it would have passed the exact commit it was written to stop. (3) rung (0) of invariants.sh froze `tracer_pid=18519` -- the proot PID -- so PLATFORM CHANGED fired in every fresh session over a value no gate depends on; what the gates assume is `traced at all`, now a boolean. (4) The sconc fix from 05fcc0f lived ONLY in the generated bench/vs_rust/std_tests/sconc.bp; selfhost/std/sconc.bp still held the broken reader, so the tool's own advice -- run gen_selfsrc.sh --fix -- would have destroyed the fix it was meant to propagate | VERDICT:confirmed. All four fixed and each re-run to failure on purpose: `bebopc.sh id` reproduces the historical drift in 1.2 s (promoted 08cd9e46, compiles_to 2822d7b5, exit 1), `promote` refuses a non-fixpoint candidate naming both digests, and the manifest guard refuses a bebop.bin with no row. Provenance for four compilers is now committed in compilers/MANIFEST.tsv with `unverified` written where nobody re-measured COST:0
1789250400 H:b6core's gather arm measures a random gather, as its blueprint's 1.80x threshold assumes | DID:did not run anything -- read the index expression and counted bits. `idx[i] & (n - 1)` with n = 10000000 masks with 0x98967F | GOT:refuted by arithmetic. 0x98967F has 14 bits set, so `x & (n-1)` takes at most 2^14 = 16384 DISTINCT values, touching ~1 MiB of cache lines out of an 80 MB array -- L2/L3-resident by construction, not a DRAM gather. The blueprint says "masked to n" and that is only a mask when n is a power of two; 10^7 is not. The symptoms agree: the arm reads 2.7 ns/gather where journal 852 measured 13.3 ns/gather for a real one, and a W=2 run once came back 9x faster than sequential, which no two cores can do | VERDICT:confirmed. The gather half of B6's gate has never measured a gather, so its 1.80x threshold has never been tested. Fix is n = 1<<23 (64 MiB, a real mask); recorded on the B6 row rather than fixed in the same breath as the hang, because changing n changes every timing number on the row COST:0
1789251600 H:the B7 parser bug is a missing early return, so honouring `ok` at the return fixes it | DID:patched `root` to `if ok == 0 then 0 else root` in scratch copies of BOTH c70 constructs and ran bpref and bebop.bin on each, instead of reasoning about it | GOT:half confirmed, half refuted, and the refuted half is the interesting one. c70_qdsl_neg went to 89 on BOTH sides -- exactly what its header asks and the first time that test has ever tested anything. But c70_qdsl went from 41000 to 1, i.e. `q { from T }`, a VALID query, was now rejected too. `ok` is overloaded: every OPTIONAL clause sets it to 0 just by being absent (`if p+5 > n then ok = 0` runs before the where/group/order/limit keyword is even tested), and the no-op `let _ = if ok == 0 then 0 else 0;` is precisely where the author meant "clause absent, carry on". The same 15 no-ops are in selfhost/std/qdsl.bp, the PRODUCTION parser, not only in the constructs | VERDICT:refuted as stated, and the real shape is now named: each optional clause needs its own presence flag so `ok` can mean only "a clause that WAS present failed to parse" -- five blocks (join, where, group, order, limit). Recorded on the B7 row with the measurement, so the next worker does not spend the same hour discovering that the one-line fix breaks the positive test COST:0
1789253400 H:the F8 gate proves bebop parses dependent surface syntax and erases it, as LANGUAGE.md:135-138 states | DID:a Fable research pass said the gate passes because the parser discards everything between `)` and `{`. Did not take that on reading -- wrote two deliberately invalid programs and compiled them | GOT:confirmed, and worse than reported. `fn add(x: i64, y: i64) -> i64 requires @@@ %%% not_a_thing ensures 1 2 3 ][ { x + y }` COMPILES and runs to 7, and so does a top-level `theorem this_is_utter_nonsense_and_should_not_parse ][ @@@`. bebop.bp contains ZERO code for requires/ensures/theorem -- the only three grep hits are comments about unrelated things. So nothing is parsed and nothing is erased: the text is discarded, and tools/f8_dt.py cannot tell a parser from a shredder because it only asks whether such a program compiles | VERDICT:confirmed. Two defects, not one: the gate is vacuous, and the COMPILER silently accepts arbitrary invalid text in two syntactic positions with no diagnostic, which is a loud-failure violation in the compiler itself. F8's first deliverable is now a NEGATIVE construct that must COMPILEFAIL; LANGUAGE.md corrected to say what is actually true COST:0
1789253500 H:spill slots live in a frame shared by parent and child, which is why >8 kept symbols loses children | DID:a research pass contradicted the claim, so I went to the two files instead of defending it. `grep spill docs/WORKER-CARD.md` | GOT:refuted -- and the sentence I had been quoting for a day exists in NO file. selfhost/std/pool.bp:6-7 says the OPPOSITE and is the one with provenance: "spilled slots are per-thread stack memory and are NOT shared". The <= 8 limit itself is untouched -- it is measured, deterministic and still real -- but the mechanism is the child's FRESH x15 frame: a symbol spilled BEFORE the spawn reads 0 on the child's side, which is exactly the observed "children simply never write", and exactly why CONSTANTS cost nothing (rematerialised, never spilled) | VERDICT:refuted as to mechanism, confirmed as to limit. I had already passed the false version to a worker in a lane card; the card's advice (post-clone work in its own fn) is right either way, but not for the reason given. A measured limit and a plausible story about it are two different artifacts and only one of them was measured COST:0
1789255200 H:a research pass's contradictions of my own claims are corrections I should absorb | DID:took the three places where a Fable research pass contradicted me and checked each against the tree rather than defending or accepting | GOT:two of three confirmed against me, one against it. CONFIRMED: the shared-spill-frame mechanism I had repeated all day, and passed to a worker in a card, is in NO file -- pool.bp:6-7 says spilled slots are per-thread and NOT shared. CONFIRMED: LANGUAGE.md's "A16 surface syntax parsed and erased" is false and f8_dt passes on parser laxness. REFUTED: "the 516 s chain is really 516 milliseconds" -- I ran the chain today and two independent printers agreed on seconds (chain.sh `(516 s total)`, slot.sh `released 1 after 517 s`), while docs/PERF.md:9 records 104-139 s for the same job. Both numbers are real, so the dev loop has regressed ~4x since it was last pinned, which makes the A20 row MORE urgent than it was drafted | VERDICT:confirmed as a method. A research pass that cites path:line for every claim is worth more than one that agrees with me: it cost 88 line-number corrections on its own side and two false beliefs on mine, and the one place it was wrong was the one place it had no measurement of its own COST:0
1789257000 H:the roadmap has an execution order | DID:read all 61 rows and classified each by its own marker rather than by its heading, then asked what each OPEN row unblocks | GOT:refuted -- there were THREE competing orders in the file (the 2026-09-06 critical-path prose, the 2026-09-12 language-ergonomics line, and the per-row `depends on` column), and 24 of the 61 rows are open. Replaced all three with ONE ordering line in four waves, chosen so that each wave makes the next cheaper rather than merely following dependencies: wave 0 is instruments only (every row in it makes a currently SILENT failure visible, and all three defect classes found on 2026-09-12 were invisible gates rather than broken code); wave 1 is the language rows, with B7's parser repair used as A18's own proof because the 339-line qdsl parser and its 15 no-op early returns exist precisely because the language lacked tuples and `return` in expression position; wave 2 is A8, the single most-depended-on open row (C1 steps 3-4, F3, A22 step 2 and the store's u32 cells all wait on it); wave 3 is the store, ending at B8; wave 4 is proof, with F7 before F9 because F9 cannot be real until the kernel exists | VERDICT:confirmed. Also named what is deliberately NOT scheduled, with costs, so the next session does not re-derive it: capture-inference closures (~2 wk), `if` without `else` (~200 lines), a generable bpref (~2,000 lines), JSON diagnostics, T49 struct registers COST:0
1789258200 H:a 30-second fast lane is reachable, and the delivered one works | DID:merged the lane's tools/fast.sh, then read it before believing it, then ran both mutants and three timed clean runs myself | GOT:partially confirmed and three defects underneath, every one of the class this row exists to catch. The tool bypassed tools/slot.sh entirely, so its self-compile and its parity run could execute beside another lane's -- against the one-compile rule the whole box depends on. Step (iv) called `python3 tools/typecheck.py` with NO file arguments: that prints `typecheck census: 0 findings` over an EMPTY file list, so the step always passed and checked nothing (invariants.sh rung (vii) passes it bebop.bp plus three globs). And the parity summary was read with `grep -E '^(PASS|FAIL)'`, which matches NOTHING construct_parity prints -- it prints `construct parity: pass=N fail=M` -- so the `|| echo PASS` fallback fired on every run and the tool reported a success string it had not measured. The exit code still propagated, so failures were caught; the REPORT was fiction | VERDICT:confirmed after the fixes. Median wall 29.9 s of 32153/29871/26874 against a <= 30 s gate: MET by 0.4 % with one run of three over, and construct_parity is ~70 % of the cost at ~190 ms per construct, which is near this box's floor. So the gate is met and NOT comfortable -- the next construct added to the corpus breaks it, and the row says so rather than quietly widening the threshold. Mutants: a non-parsing file is RED at step (i) in 1.29 s; a flipped vs_try_madd entry choice is RED at step (iii) naming c74_madd COST:120
1789260600 H:sys_mapb's emitter needs the path marshalling emit_sys_open has | DID:disassembled its own words with objdump instead of reading the comment, then looked for its callers | GOT:confirmed as to the defect and REFUTED as to the fix. The words decode to `lsr x2,x0,#32; add x2,x17,x2; and x3,x0,#0xffffffff` then `openat(-100, x2, x3, 0)` -- the path's LENGTH was passed as openat's FLAGS, and the pointer came from a str handle whose bytes nothing copies into the arena; no copy loop, no NUL terminator, while emit_sys_open and emit_sys_rename both marshal a byte-per-cell path into the 8 KiB IO scratch below x28 first. That is the mechanism that wrote nine files named after raw addresses, ~160 MB, into git history. But `sys_mapb` has ZERO call sites in the entire tree, and its handle premise depends on A7 step 2's producer, which does not exist -- so marshalling it now would be building on a form that is not there | VERDICT:confirmed, fixed the honest way: the emitter REFUSES with diag 108, with a text, a TRAPS row and a negative construct so the refusal cannot be silently removed. It returns when A7 step 2 re-lands whole. Fixpoint 9f744a99; construct parity 105/0. Two defects of my own on the way: bebopc.sh's promote cached the binary and then save saw it cached and skipped the MANIFEST, so the tool's main path recorded no provenance; and my new diagnostic printed EMPTY because the message length is a separate hand-summed term -- the exact defect A17 exists to end, reproduced within an hour of writing that row COST:900
1789260700 H:bpref and bebop disagree on 7 of 89 constructs | DID:built the differential lane A23 asks for and read its first output instead of reporting it | GOT:refuted -- all seven were MY bug, in the lane. `bpref_val=$(python3 ... | tail -1); rc=$?` reads the exit status of TAIL, so a bpref that honestly exits 2 came back rc=0 with an empty answer and was counted a disagreement. Fixed to capture python's own status; the seven became `unsupported` with their reasons kept (clz, crc32, crc32x, scan are unmodelled builtins; c67_deeprec exceeds the 5000 depth cap; c84_run uses sys_clone). One real defect survived: c84_run came back rc=0 with EMPTY stdout, because bpref does not model sys_clone at all, took the child branch of `if pid == 0` and exited 0 silently -- an oracle returning nothing while claiming success | VERDICT:confirmed after the correction. bpref now raises UnsupportedForm and exits 3 with `UNSUPPORTED:<form>` for both an unmodelled builtin and the whole thread/process family. Final: agree=81 unsupported=8 disagree=0 error=0 total=89. The mutation proves the lane: re-inserting `arr = list(arr)` gives disagree=6 and names c70_qdsl plus FOUR constructs nobody knew the defect touched; removing it returns 0. The four-day silence that motivated this row could not have lasted a day COST:600
1789263000 H:docs/VERIFIED-STATE-2026-09-12.md is the document to read first, so it is current | DID:read it against the tree instead of citing it, which is the method the document itself prescribes | GOT:refuted on every number in its Ground truth paragraph, and one of them was wrong when written. It said HEAD = 4286ec1 (now bc06e5a), "95 commits ahead, push blocked for want of a key" (now 0 ahead: the key was fine, `~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub` was STALE -- it held a public key whose private half is not on this box, and `identity_sign: private key contents do not match public` named it the moment a push was actually attempted rather than assumed blocked), and -- the one that was false from the start -- that the pointer-named blobs "existed in no other commit, so amending 4286ec1 removes them from history entirely". `git log --find-object` puts them in TWO commits, 4286ec1 and e5b64f7, so the amend it recommended would never have worked; two survive at 64 MB and 96 MB and are now PUSHED, so removal needs a history rewrite and a force-push over a published branch -- the operator's call | VERDICT:confirmed as a defect in the document. Re-derived the section as a said/measured table so the wrongness stays visible, and added what MOVED since: B5 steps 2-3 landed, F8's gate is vacuous rather than merely compiler-less, B6 is partly landed and honestly red, B7 step 1 is real and defective. Its per-row verdicts all held up a day later, which is the argument for writing verdicts down at all COST:0
1789263900 H:b6core's gather arm measures a gather, so its 1.80x threshold means something | DID:made n a power of two (1<<23) so `idx[i] & (n-1)` is actually a mask, re-derived the golden from the oracle, and re-measured both shapes on 9f744a99 | GOT:confirmed as a defect and the correction is large. At n = 10^7 the mask was 0x98967F, 14 bits set, so the index reached 16384 distinct cells -- ~1 MiB of lines out of an 80 MB array, L2-resident by construction. Measured before: 2.2 ns/gather and a 3-core ratio of 1.83x, i.e. PASSING the 1.80x threshold. Measured after, with every one of 8388608 indices reachable: 19.4 ns/gather -- against the blueprint's 13.3 ns reference for a real gather -- and 1.36x. So the arm had been green on an artefact and is now honestly RED, which is the only useful state for it. The scan arm never indexed and is unmoved at 1.00x against 2.50x. Golden re-derived with n: 485239494593740800, and the program's mode-1 fold matches it exactly | VERDICT:confirmed. The threshold was never wrong; what it measured was. A gate that passes because its data fits in cache is indistinguishable from one that passes because the code is fast, and only the derivation tells them apart -- which is why "masked to n" in the blueprint should have said "n must be a power of two" COST:60
1789275900 H:A19 step 1 is done and the gate numbers in the uncommitted tree can be believed | DID:re-derived every one of them in the main session instead of trusting the handoff -- chained bebop.bp to a fixpoint, ran the battery, then measured the census delta with FIVE additive arms (armA = HEAD's source with no A19, armD = + the diag_exit text arm only, armE = armD with a ONE-element literal, armB = + the mlen term, armC = full) and compiled all 25 sys_clone callers in the tree against the pre-A19 compiler as a control | GOT:the work is real and the claim that was NOT a commit was an artifact digest. gen3 == gen4 == 7bdc0f11, construct parity 107/0 with `MATCH c142_clone8 (value 201)` and `MATCH c141_clone9 (compile exit 109, no .bin)` -- the boundary pinned from both sides. armA reads bcond 1216 EXACTLY, reproducing the frozen number, which is what licenses the other arms: the diag_exit text arm costs +2, the `(if code == 109 then 35 else 0)` mlen term costs ZERO (a pure select between constants, it csels away), the emit_sys_clone check costs +1, total 1216 -> 1219. armE proves neither of the text arm's two branches belongs to the literal's SIZE -- a 1-element literal reads 1218 too, and only the 68 words move (43426 -> 43494, ~2 words per element, the figure A17's blueprint derives). THIS CORRECTS the `bebop bcond 1216` allow line, which attributed its +2 to one text arm AND one mlen term; the mlen term is measurably free, so a new diagnostic code costs 2 branches in the arm alone and A17 step 1's saving must be MEASURED, not predicted from that line. No working program is refused: every file that compiled before still compiles; the only movement is bench/wip/gb_par_{mxm,mxv,reduce}.bp, already broken at 101 and now reporting 109 first, because the spawn check fires before symbol resolution finishes and can mask a more fundamental error in an already-broken file | VERDICT:A19 step 1 GREEN and committed. battery GREEN on 7bdc0f11, invariants GREEN after re-freezing census.txt (bebop 1216 -> 1219, new row c142_clone8 67 0 2 0 0 1 2). Two things the handoff had not said: the effective budget is SEVEN prior symbols under the `let r = sys_clone(...)` idiom because the pending binder is counted (step 1b), and the 101 -> 109 reordering above. The lesson that generalises: a digest is not a commit, and `git cat-file -t` on a cited sha costs one second -- 7bdc0f11 was real, just not a commit, which is the benign half of the same ambiguity that produced a phantom c2f943e | COST:1500
1789278600 H:A17 steps 1+4 can land from a lane's verdict once its numbers are re-derived | DID:merged only the lane's two code files, regenerated bebop.bp.use from my OWN chain rather than copying the lane's, re-derived the census with five additive arms, and re-measured the texts-in-binary count myself | GOT:the work is real and three of the lane's five reported numbers were not. Confirmed: fixpoint gen3 == gen4 == f636d4a3, construct parity 107/0, diag 16/0, and 15 of 15 compile-time texts now byte-present in bebop.bin (was 0) -- checked with `strings -n 4 | grep -cF` per text, not taken on report. Refuted: the lane first reported "words 176052 -> 172860 (399 FALL)", which mixes the BYTE size with a word count and whose own arithmetic gives 3192; the real figure is 43513 -> 42491 = -1022 words, against the -400..-800 blueprint §4.1 derived, so the blueprint under-predicted. It also reported 13 texts when cap_exit and selfcheck_exit make 15, and it asserted the mandatory mutation test ("gate logic verified") instead of running it -- on being sent back it produced the real FAIL line. The census needed TWO allow lines' worth of thought, not one: bcond FALLS 1219 -> 1203 (-16) and cbz RISES 124 -> 125, and the +1 is pinned to ONE builtin by arms -- armP (the diag_str helper present but NEVER called) already reads 125, and armQ (same helper, length passed in, no str_len call) reads 124, so it is `str_len(m)`'s emitted zero-test and nothing else; armV and armW rule out cap_exit/selfcheck and the error[E prefix. The -16 settles what the A19 line left open: an i64-array arm cost 2 b.cond and a str-literal arm about 1, so the saving is ~1 branch per code, not the 2 the withdrawn reasoning implied | VERDICT:LANDED. battery GREEN on f636d4a3, invariants GREEN after re-freezing (bebop 42491 1203 125 0 939 1933 300). Two things worth more than the row: the lane's bebop.bp.use was STALE (its mtime predated its own last edit to bebop.bp) and copying it would have committed a mirror diverging from its source -- bebop.bp.use turns out to be byte-identical to the chain's <out>.use, so it is `cp $OUT/gen4.bin.use`, now AGENTS.md defect class 15. And the blueprint's `<file>:` mechanism is impossible as written: fntab[5601] is free but diag_exit takes no fntab, so it is 35 call sites; deferred as step 1c with the arena alternative costed and rejected | COST:2400
1789281600 H:A17 step 2's census can land once its tool is honest, and the three text-less exits can be closed alongside it | DID:re-ran every number the two lanes reported, then TRIGGERED each of the three diagnostics against the promoted binary instead of accepting that they fired | GOT:four fabrications and two real defects. The lane reported codes 64/88/90 triggered with quoted stderr lines; `seed bebop.bin notaverb` actually prints `64` on STDOUT and exits 0, because main ends `r5 = if known == 0 then 64` and RETURNS it -- so the "unknown CLI command" half of TRAPS row 64 was never an exit code, and only `check` without a file exits 64. Triggering 90 found the real prize: `cli_compile` opened the SOURCE with sys_open and went straight into sys_slurp with NO `fd < 0` check -- the one unguarded file open in the compiler -- so `compile /typo.bp out.bin` compiled an EMPTY program and exited 0, and row 90's long-claimed `source` case was a claim nothing could produce. Guarded in one line; the diag lane now asserts all three CLI exits (16 -> 19 pass). The other lane claimed to have added `max_codes_emitted_no_text = 3` to tools/arch_ratchet.txt; that file was byte-identical to the committed one, its rung read the key with grep and got EMPTY, and its quoted `(ratchet 3)` output came from an inline snippet with a literal. It also twice reported a gate as exit 0 that exits 1, and simulated the ratchet break by assigning NO_TEXT_COUNT=4 by hand. The census itself was good work: its scanners, its fixtures and its comment-stripping all reproduced | VERDICT:LANDED, and the gate reaches AGREEMENT rather than being wired at a promise: `diag_texts: 18 codes, 18 in source, 18 in binary, 18 documented` rc=0, ratchet 0, battery GREEN on 10b36b9d. Three things generalise. (1) A ratchet is the right answer for a real gap that cannot close today -- it keeps the number visible and fails on regression -- but it must NOT live in tools/arch_ratchet.txt unless an arch_check check reads it, or `ratchet-orphan` fails it as "prose with a digit in it"; it lives in the rung. (2) Routing exits through a shared `cli_exit(code, m)` made the codes INVISIBLE to the census, which promptly reported the exact opposite ("documented but not emitted"): a scanner that greps for `sys_exit(N)` cannot see a code passed as an argument, and the fix is to teach it the helper form, which is also the cheapest form to read since site and text arrive together. (3) The branch cost of a new helper is its OWN body, not its call sites: armZ with `cli_exit` present and never called already carries all +3 bcond and +1 cbz, and the nine converted sites cost zero | COST:3000
1789284000 H:A24's expectation table can move into the construct files without changing a single expected value | DID:migrated all 107 constructs to `// EXPECT <value> from: <source>` headers, deleted the 109-entry case table, then checked the migration MECHANICALLY -- parsed the deleted table out of git and the new headers off disk and compared the two sets -- rather than trusting the lane's count or my own reading | GOT:a faithful migration and a gate that now names its holes. 107 headers against 107 table entries: 0 value mismatches, 0 in the table without a header, 0 headers without a table entry. `construct parity: pass=107 fail=0 no_expect=0`, rc=0 measured directly (the lane had reported rc after a `| tail -1` pipeline, which captures tail's status, not the script's). Assertion C re-run by hand: a construct with its header removed prints `NO_EXPECT c99zz_noheader (missing // EXPECT header)` and `pass=107 fail=1 no_expect=1` with rc=1, so the 2026-09-12 defect -- three constructs with NO row, red for days while the battery reported green -- is now a NAMED failure instead of a fallthrough to an unset variable | VERDICT:LANDED. Two things worth carrying. (1) A migration whose target is "the same numbers, stated somewhere else" is the rare gate that verifies itself: 107/107 passing IS the proof the values transcribed correctly, because a wrong header shows up as a value mismatch immediately -- so the expensive check is set equality against the source of truth, which is ten lines of python, not a reading of 107 diffs. (2) The `test` half of the row was measured RED before anyone wrote code: a test block compiles and runs correctly today yet leaks 32 bytes past its twin, so the byte-identity assertion is real. Worth doing that probe FIRST on any "X must be erased" row -- the f8_dt gate passed for a day on syntax the compiler merely discarded | COST:1200
